Composition and Layout Components

Notes from EpicWeb’s Advanced React Patterns
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 1, “Composition”) plus his blog posts
Prop Drilling and
One React mistake that’s slowing you down.
Companion to Compound Components and The Slots Pattern, which build
on the same “pass elements, not data” idea. Notes and clarifications by me
are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What problem does the composition/layout-components pattern solve?
A: Prop drilling — passing a prop through components that don’t use it
themselves, purely so a descendant further down the tree can use it. In our
example, user, sportList, selectedSport, and setSelectedSport all
have to pass through App → Nav/Main/Footer even though those three
components never read them; only their children (avatar, SportListItemButton,
Details) actually need the data.
Q: What’s the fix, concretely?
A: Stop passing data down and pass React elements down instead. A
component that only lays things out — decides where on the page something
renders, not what it is — takes React.ReactNode props (or children)
instead of the raw data its descendants need. The parent that already has
the data builds the finished element once, at the top, and hands it down as
a prop:
// ❌ Nav has to accept and forward a prop it never uses
function Nav({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return (
    &lt;nav&gt;
      {/* ...links... */}
      &lt;a href=&quot;#/me&quot;&gt;&lt;img src={user.image} alt={`${user.name} profile`} /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/nav&gt;
  )
}

// ✅ Nav only cares about laying out an avatar — it doesn&#039;t care what it is
function Nav({ avatar }: { avatar: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    &lt;nav&gt;
      {/* ...links... */}
      &lt;a href=&quot;#/me&quot; title=&quot;User Settings&quot;&gt;{avatar}&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/nav&gt;
  )
}

// App builds the element once, where the data actually lives
&lt;Nav avatar={&lt;img src={user.image} alt={`${user.name} profile`} /&gt;} /&gt;
Q: Doesn’t children already solve this?
A: For a single slot, yes — children is exactly this pattern applied to
one particular prop name. The workshop’s Main component is the case
children can’t cover on its own: it needs to lay out two independent
regions (a sidebar and the main content), so it takes two explicitly named
ReactNode props instead of one children:
function Main({
  sidebar,
  content,
}: {
  sidebar: React.ReactNode
  content: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return (
    &lt;main&gt;
      {sidebar}
      {content}
    &lt;/main&gt;
  )
}
Q: Why does this reduce prop drilling instead of just moving it?
A: Because the element is constructed where the data lives, not merely
forwarded through components that don’t use it. Nav, Main, and
Footer never see user, sportList, or setSelectedSport at all in the
fixed version — they only see finished ReactNodes. If a new field is later
added to User, only App (and whatever renders the avatar) needs to
change; Nav’s signature is untouched. In the prop-drilled version, every
intermediate component’s prop types would need to grow too.
Q: Isn’t this just React context with extra steps?
A: No — it’s an alternative to reaching for context, not a rebrand of it.
Context is for data many unrelated branches of the tree need implicitly
(theme, auth user, locale). Composition is for the much more common case:
one specific piece of UI needs to be placed somewhere by a component that
doesn’t otherwise care about it. Kent’s framing (from the blog posts above):
reach for more composition before reaching for context — restructuring
components to accept elements instead of data solves most “prop drilling”
pain without adding a Provider, a hook, or re-render fan-out to everything
that consumes the context.
Q: When does this pattern stop paying off?
A: When you’re threading so many named ReactNode slots through a
“layout” component that the prop list becomes its own kind of noise — the
workshop README calls this out explicitly (“clearly you can take this too
far”). If a layout component needs many independent regions, that’s often a
sign to reach for the more structured version of this same idea:
The Slots Pattern (context-based slot registration) or
Compound Components (implicit wiring via React.Children/context)
instead of an ever-growing prop list.
The whole example, before and after
Before (data drilled through Nav, Main, Footer, none of which use it):
function App() {
  const [user] = useState&lt;User&gt;(...)
  const [sportList] = useState&lt;Array&lt;SportData&gt;&gt;(...)
  const [selectedSport, setSelectedSport] = useState&lt;SportData | null&gt;(null)
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Nav user={user} /&gt;
      &lt;Main sportList={sportList} selectedSport={selectedSport} setSelectedSport={setSelectedSport} /&gt;
      &lt;Footer user={user} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
After (elements built at the top, components only lay them out):
function App() {
  const [user] = useState&lt;User&gt;({ name: &#039;Kody&#039;, image: &#039;/img/kody.png&#039; })
  const [sportList] = useState&lt;Array&lt;SportData&gt;&gt;(() =&gt; Object.values(allSports))
  const [selectedSport, setSelectedSport] = useState&lt;SportData | null&gt;(null)

  return (
    &lt;div id=&quot;app-root&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Nav avatar={&lt;img src={user.image} alt={`${user.name} profile`} /&gt;} /&gt;
      &lt;Main
        sidebar={
          &lt;List
            listItems={sportList.map((p) =&gt; (
              &lt;li key={p.id}&gt;
                &lt;SportListItemButton sport={p} onClick={() =&gt; setSelectedSport(p)} /&gt;
              &lt;/li&gt;
            ))}
          /&gt;
        }
        content={&lt;Details selectedSport={selectedSport} /&gt;}
      /&gt;
      &lt;Footer footerMessage={`Don&#039;t have a good day–have a great day, ${user.name}`} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

function Nav({ avatar }: { avatar: React.ReactNode }) { /* only lays out avatar */ }
function Main({ sidebar, content }: { sidebar: React.ReactNode; content: React.ReactNode }) { /* only lays out two regions */ }
function Footer({ footerMessage }: { footerMessage: string }) { /* only renders a string it&#039;s handed */ }
SportListItemButton still receives sport and onClick directly — it’s
not a layout component, it’s the component that actually uses that data,
so passing it as normal props is correct. The pattern is specifically for
the components in between that don’t use what they’re forwarding.
Rules and caveats

Only apply this to components that are purely structural. A component
that reads and branches on the data it receives (like Details branching
on whether selectedSport is set) should keep taking that data as a real
prop — converting it to also take a ReactNode would just hide logic
behind an opaque element.
children is the special case of one slot. Prefer plain children
over a single named ReactNode prop when there’s genuinely only one
region to fill (idiomatic React, and what most library components do);
reach for named ReactNode props only when a component lays out more than
one independent region, as Main does here.
This doesn’t remove state — it relocates where elements are built.
selectedSport/setSelectedSport still live in App; composition only
changes how the pieces that read/write it get handed to their eventual
render location, not where the state itself lives.
It composes with the other patterns in this workshop. [[The Slots Pattern]]
is this same idea formalized with context so deeply nested consumers don’t
need an unbroken chain of ReactNode props; [[Compound Components]] is
this same idea with implicit wiring (children discover their shared
state via context) instead of explicit props.

Sources

EpicWeb Advanced React Patterns workshop, exercise 01.composition (step
“compose”)
Kent C. Dodds — Prop Drilling
Kent C. Dodds — One React mistake that’s slowing you down

State Collections and Prop Getters

Notes from EpicWeb’s Advanced React Patterns
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 5, “Prop Collections and Getters”)
plus his blog post
How to give rendering control to users with prop getters.
Companion to Compound Components and The Slots Pattern — this
exercise deliberately drops down from a component-based API to a
hook-based one to show the same “give consumers flexibility without
forcing boilerplate on them” goal at a lower level of abstraction. Notes
and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What problem does a prop collection solve?
A: Repetition and easy-to-miss accessibility wiring. A toggle button needs
both aria-checked (set from state) and onClick (call toggle) to
actually work and be accessible — two things every consumer of the hook has
to remember to wire up correctly, every time. A prop collection is just an
object of the props a typical consumer needs, bundled by the hook so it can
be spread onto an element in one line:
export function useToggle() {
  const [on, setOn] = useState(false)
  const toggle = () =&gt; setOn(!on)

  return {
    on,
    toggle,
    togglerProps: {
      &#039;aria-checked&#039;: on,
      onClick: toggle,
    },
  }
}

function App() {
  const { on, togglerProps } = useToggle()
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Switch on={on} {...togglerProps} /&gt;
      &lt;hr /&gt;
      &lt;button aria-label=&quot;custom-button&quot; {...togglerProps}&gt;
        {on ? &#039;on&#039; : &#039;off&#039;}
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Q: What breaks when a consumer wants to add their own onClick alongside togglerProps?
A: Object spread order decides the winner, silently. {...togglerProps} onClick={...}
would let the consumer’s handler replace the hook’s — the toggle would
stop working, with no error, because a plain object is just data; spreading
it has no way to know “this key should be merged, not overwritten.” This is
the exact break the workshop has you hit before fixing it: adding
onClick={() =&gt; console.info(&#039;onButtonClick&#039;)} next to {...togglerProps}
either kills the toggle behavior or kills the consumer’s log, depending on
spread order — never both.
Q: How do prop getters fix that?
A: By turning the props object into a function call instead of a static
value, so the hook — not object-spread order — decides how to combine its
own props with the consumer’s:
function callAll&lt;Args extends Array&lt;unknown&gt;&gt;(
  ...fns: Array&lt;((...args: Args) =&gt; unknown) | undefined&gt;
) {
  return (...args: Args) =&gt; fns.forEach((fn) =&gt; fn?.(...args))
}

export function useToggle() {
  const [on, setOn] = useState(false)
  const toggle = () =&gt; setOn(!on)

  function getTogglerProps&lt;Props&gt;({
    onClick,
    ...props
  }: { onClick?: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;button&#039;&gt;[&#039;onClick&#039;] } &amp; Props) {
    return {
      &#039;aria-checked&#039;: on,
      onClick: callAll(onClick, toggle),
      ...props,
    }
  }

  return { on, toggle, getTogglerProps }
}

function App() {
  const { on, getTogglerProps } = useToggle()
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Switch {...getTogglerProps({ on })} /&gt;
      &lt;hr /&gt;
      &lt;button
        {...getTogglerProps({
          &#039;aria-label&#039;: &#039;custom-button&#039;,
          onClick: () =&gt; console.info(&#039;onButtonClick&#039;),
          id: &#039;custom-button-id&#039;,
        })}
      &gt;
        {on ? &#039;on&#039; : &#039;off&#039;}
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Both onClicks now run — toggle() (the hook’s own behavior) and the
consumer’s () =&gt; console.info(&#039;onButtonClick&#039;) — every time the button is
clicked, in the order callAll lists them.
Q: Why is onClick destructured out specially instead of just spread with everything else?
A: Because it’s the one prop that needs merging logic (run both
functions), while every other prop the consumer passes (id,
aria-label, …) should just pass through untouched. getTogglerProps
destructures onClick to hand it to callAll alongside the hook’s own
toggle, then spreads the rest of props — including a caller-supplied
aria-checked, if they really wanted to override that — last, so
non-function overrides still win where that’s the sensible default.
Q: Isn’t this the same idea as element.addEventListener
A: Conceptually yes — both let multiple independent pieces of code react to
the same event without one silently replacing another. addEventListener
does it at the DOM API level (multiple listeners, all invoked); callAll
does the equivalent for a single React prop that can only hold one function,
by composing N functions into 1 before it’s ever assigned to that prop.
Q: When do you reach for a prop collection versus a prop getter?
A: A collection is enough when consumers will only ever spread it as-is —
simple, no function call needed. Reach for a getter the moment you expect
consumers to want to supply their own handler alongside the hook’s — which
in practice is often, so libraries built around this pattern (downshift,
react-toggled, conform’s getFormProps) default to getters everywhere,
even for props that don’t strictly need merging yet, for API consistency.
Q: Why drop the Toggle compound component from earlier exercises for a plain hook here?
A: The workshop calls this out explicitly: prop collections/getters are a
lower-level API than compound components. Dropping to the hook gives
consumers full control over which element gets which props (an App can
apply togglerProps/getTogglerProps to a &lt;Switch&gt; and an unrelated
custom &lt;button&gt;, both driven by the same on state) — something a
&lt;Toggle&gt;&lt;ToggleButton/&gt;&lt;/Toggle&gt; compound-component API doesn’t expose as
directly. Nothing stops combining them: a Toggle component can be
re-built on top of useToggle, giving consumers both the convenient
declarative form and the raw hook for custom UI (see Kent’s
Mixing Component Patterns).
How the pattern actually works, end to end
Why this exists: the accessibility-wiring problem
The workshop’s own framing is worth stating in full, because it’s the
motivating case for the whole pattern, not just a throwaway example: a
toggle button needs aria-checked set correctly and an onClick that
calls toggle for it to be both functional and accessible. Neither of
those is optional, and neither is obvious from the outside — a consumer of
useToggle has to already know both requirements and remember to wire up
both, every single time they render a togglable element. Kent’s own
useCounter example makes the shape of the fix concrete before the
workshop’s more involved useToggle version:
function useCounter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(initialCount)
  const increment = () =&gt; setCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)
  return {
    count,
    increment,
    counterButtonProps: { children: count, onClick: increment }, // the prop collection
  }
}

function App() {
  const counter = useCounter()
  return &lt;button {...counter.counterButtonProps} /&gt;
}
The hook, not the consumer, now owns “what props does a typical button
driven by this hook need” — bundled as one object, spreadable in one line.
This is a prop collection: nothing more than an object of props, built
once per render by the hook and handed back alongside its normal return
values.
Where it breaks: the override problem
A prop collection is a plain object, and objects have no idea what a
“handler” is versus any other value — spreading one is just
last-key-wins. The workshop deliberately has you hit this before fixing
it: take the working togglerProps object and try to add your own
onClick alongside it:
&lt;button
  aria-label=&quot;custom-button&quot;
  {...togglerProps}
  onClick={() =&gt; console.info(&#039;onButtonClick&#039;)}
&gt;
  {on ? &#039;on&#039; : &#039;off&#039;}
&lt;/button&gt;
Whichever onClick appears later in the spread order wins outright — here,
the consumer’s own handler silently replaces the hook’s toggle call,
so the button stops actually toggling, with no error or warning anywhere.
Swap the order and the opposite breaks: the hook’s own toggle runs, but
the consumer’s logging handler never does. There’s no ordering of a plain
object spread that lets both survive, because JS object spread doesn’t
know these two values are both “the onClick handler that should run” —
they’re just two writes to the same key.
Kent’s blog post “How to give rendering control to users with prop
getters”
frames this as a general tension in component/hook API design: you want to
hand as much rendering control to the consumer as possible (which handler
runs, what markup wraps it, what other props get attached) without that
control being able to silently break the thing your hook is actually
responsible for. His summary of the fix’s philosophy is the cleanest
statement of the whole pattern’s purpose: “your components take care of
the hard and generic part (the logic of the component) and the user can
take care of the easy and less-generic part: what to show and how it’s
styled” — the prop getter is the seam between those two responsibilities.
The fix: turn the props object into a function
Once “combine my handler with the consumer’s” is the actual requirement,
a static object can’t do it — only code that runs at call time can decide
how to combine two functions. That’s the entire idea behind a prop
getter: replace the object with a function that accepts the consumer’s
own props (including their own onClick, if any) and returns the merged
result:
function callAll&lt;Args extends Array&lt;unknown&gt;&gt;(
  ...fns: Array&lt;((...args: Args) =&gt; unknown) | undefined&gt;
) {
  return (...args: Args) =&gt; fns.forEach((fn) =&gt; fn?.(...args))
}

export function useToggle() {
  const [on, setOn] = useState(false)
  const toggle = () =&gt; setOn(!on)

  function getTogglerProps&lt;Props&gt;({
    onClick,
    ...props
  }: { onClick?: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;button&#039;&gt;[&#039;onClick&#039;] } &amp; Props) {
    return {
      &#039;aria-checked&#039;: on,
      onClick: callAll(onClick, toggle),
      ...props,
    }
  }

  return { on, toggle, getTogglerProps }
}
callAll is a small but important piece of machinery on its own: it takes
any number of possibly-undefined functions and returns one function that
calls every one of them, in order, ignoring the ones that are undefined.
That’s what makes onClick: callAll(onClick, toggle) correct even when
the consumer passes no onClick at all — callAll(undefined, toggle)
still just calls toggle. The consumer now calls getTogglerProps instead
of spreading a static object, handing it whatever extra props (including
their own onClick) they want applied:
function App() {
  const { on, getTogglerProps } = useToggle()
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Switch {...getTogglerProps({ on })} /&gt;
      &lt;hr /&gt;
      &lt;button
        {...getTogglerProps({
          &#039;aria-label&#039;: &#039;custom-button&#039;,
          onClick: () =&gt; console.info(&#039;onButtonClick&#039;),
          id: &#039;custom-button-id&#039;,
        })}
      &gt;
        {on ? &#039;on&#039; : &#039;off&#039;}
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Both buttons now behave correctly: the &lt;Switch&gt; gets the hook’s own
props verbatim (no user onClick to merge — callAll(undefined, toggle)
just runs toggle); the custom &lt;button&gt; gets both its own onClick
(the console log) and the hook’s toggle call, every click, because
getTogglerProps composed them at the moment it built the returned props
object rather than leaving that to object-spread order.
The real-world shape of this pattern
Every library the workshop cites as “real world projects that use this
pattern” —
downshift and
conform’s getFormProps —
converges on the same get&lt;X&gt;Props(userProps) naming and shape shown
above, which is why it’s worth learning as a named, recognizable API
convention rather than a one-off trick: seeing getInputProps,
getMenuProps, getItemProps in a library’s docs is an immediate signal
“this hook composes handlers for you; call this instead of spreading a
static object of props.”
Rules and caveats

Getters, not collections, are the safer default once any handler is
involved. A static prop-collection object can’t protect a consumer from
clobbering a function prop; a getter can. Reach for a plain collection
only for props you’re confident nobody will need to override with their
own function.
callAll silently drops nothing — it just runs everything. If the
hook’s own handler needs to run conditionally based on what the
consumer’s handler decided (e.g. preventDefault()), callAll as written
doesn’t support that — every function always runs. That’s a deliberate
simplicity trade-off in the workshop version, not a hidden bug, but worth
knowing if a caller’s handler needs veto power.
Type the destructured handler generically, as getTogglerProps does
with React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;button&#039;&gt;[&#039;onClick&#039;] — this keeps the getter
usable for both a real &lt;button&gt; and other elements (like the workshop’s
&lt;Switch&gt;) without hand-rolling the handler’s type.
This pattern composes with the others in this workshop. Compound Components
and The Slots Pattern solve “where does this go in the tree”; prop
getters solve “how do I let a consumer attach arbitrary extra behavior to
an element I’m handing them props for” — a hook can (and libraries like
downshift do) offer both a compound-component-style API and raw
prop-getters for the same underlying state.

