Slots — Render Trace (Exercise 4)

Line-by-line render/execution trace through the 3 solution steps of
exercise 4 (“Slots”) of EpicWeb’s
Advanced React Patterns
workshop by Kent C. Dodds. For the concept explanation (not the trace)
see The Slots Pattern — this note is about invocation order: when
the slot map is built, when context is read, and how a slot prop changes
which props merge into which element.

context — Slot Context
Problem: a &lt;label&gt;/&lt;input&gt; pair need a shared, generated id, but
the consumer should be free to place other markup between them — so the
id can’t be threaded as an ordinary prop the way [[Composition and Layout Components]]
would.
// slots.tsx
type Slots = Record&lt;string, Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;&gt;
export const SlotContext = createContext&lt;Slots&gt;({})

function useSlotProps&lt;Props&gt;(props: Props, slot: string): Props {
  const slots = use(SlotContext)
  return { ...slots[slot], slot, ...props } as Props
}

export function Label(props: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;label&#039;&gt;) {
  props = useSlotProps(props, &#039;label&#039;)
  return &lt;label {...props} /&gt;
}
export function Input(props: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;input&#039;&gt;) {
  props = useSlotProps(props, &#039;input&#039;)
  return &lt;input {...props} /&gt;
}

// text-field.tsx
export function TextField({ id, children }: { id?: string; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const generatedId = useId()
  id ??= generatedId
  const slots = { label: { htmlFor: id }, input: { id } }
  return &lt;SlotContext value={slots}&gt;{children}&lt;/SlotContext&gt;
}
Render trace

TextField() runs. useId() produces a stable id for this component
instance; since no id prop was passed, id ??= generatedId adopts it.
Still inside TextField, a plain object slots = { label: {...}, input: {...} }
is built once per render and handed to &lt;SlotContext value={slots}&gt;.
This is a fresh object every render (no useMemo), same as any other
context value — fine here since id only changes if the TextField
itself re-renders for another reason.
React renders TextField’s children — whatever arrangement of
&lt;Label&gt;/&lt;Input&gt;/other markup the consumer wrote, nested however deep.
Label (and Input) each call useSlotProps(props, &#039;label&#039; | &#039;input&#039;).
Inside, use(SlotContext) reads the nearest provider — the one
TextField just rendered — and returns slots[&#039;label&#039;] (or [&#039;input&#039;]).
useSlotProps merges: { ...slots[slot], slot, ...props }. Because the
caller’s own props spread last, anything the consumer explicitly
passed to &lt;Label htmlFor=&quot;custom&quot;&gt; would win over the slot-provided
htmlFor — the slot only fills in what wasn’t already specified.
Label/Input spread the merged props onto real &lt;label&gt;/&lt;input&gt;
elements. Because both pulled from the same slots.label.htmlFor /
slots.input.id — both ultimately sourced from the one id computed in
step 1 — they end up correctly associated regardless of how deeply nested
each one is inside TextField’s children.

Why
Unlike `cloneElement`-based compound components,
nothing here walks children looking for specific element types — Label
and Input find their props by reading context wherever they happen to be
rendered. That’s what makes “consumer can nest however they like” true: the
lookup is by position in the provider tree (nearest SlotContext
ancestor), not by position in the children array.
generic — Generic Slot Components
Problem: Toggle’s button currently gets its id/htmlFor wired up
manually with useId() in the consumer’s code. Reuse the same Label
component TextField uses, by having Toggle provide its own SlotContext
alongside its existing ToggleContext.
export function Toggle({ id, children }: { id?: string; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [on, setOn] = useState(false)
  const generatedId = useId()
  id ??= generatedId
  const toggle = () =&gt; setOn(!on)
  const slots = { label: { htmlFor: id } }

  return (
    &lt;SlotContext value={slots}&gt;
      &lt;ToggleContext value={{ on, toggle, id }}&gt;{children}&lt;/ToggleContext&gt;
    &lt;/SlotContext&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

Toggle() runs, computes id the same way TextField did (step 1
above), and builds two context values this time: a slots object for
SlotContext and a { on, toggle, id } object for ToggleContext.
Both providers wrap children — SlotContext outermost, ToggleContext
inside it. Order between siblings-in-nesting like these two doesn’t
matter for correctness (each consumer hook reads only the context it
cares about), only that both are ancestors of children.
&lt;Label&gt; inside &lt;Toggle&gt; resolves its label slot exactly as in the
context step — same Label component, same useSlotProps call, just a
different nearest SlotContext provider (Toggle’s instead of
TextField’s).
ToggleButton (unchanged from exercise 3) calls useToggle() →
use(ToggleContext) → gets { on, toggle, id } and renders the Switch
with that id directly — it doesn’t go through SlotContext at all in
this step, only Label does.

Why
This step establishes that a component can be the root of more than one
context tree at once (Toggle provides both SlotContext and
ToggleContext here) without those trees interfering — each consumer only
subscribes to (and re-renders on changes to) the specific context it reads.
It’s also the first demonstration that the same slot-consuming component
(Label) works unmodified across two structurally unrelated component
families, which is the whole reuse payoff of routing props through context
instead of hardcoding them per family.
prop — Slot Prop
Problem: ToggleOn, ToggleOff, and ToggleButton are three
dedicated components that each existed only to inject one specific prop
bundle. Collapse them into a generic Text (with an explicit slot prop)
and a generic Switch.
function useSlotProps&lt;Props&gt;(props: Props &amp; { slot?: string }, defaultSlot?: string): Props {
  const slot = props.slot ?? defaultSlot
  if (!slot) return props
  const slots = use(SlotContext)
  return { ...slots[slot], slot, ...props } as Props
}

export function Text(props: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;span&#039;&gt; &amp; { slot?: string }) {
  props = useSlotProps(props, &#039;text&#039;)
  return &lt;span {...props} /&gt;
}

// toggle.tsx — ToggleContext is gone; everything routes through SlotContext
export function Toggle({ id, children }: { id?: string; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [on, setOn] = useState(false)
  const generatedId = useId()
  id ??= generatedId
  const toggle = () =&gt; setOn(!on)

  return (
    &lt;SlotContext
      value={{
        label: { htmlFor: id },
        onText: { hidden: on ? undefined : true },
        offText: { hidden: on ? true : undefined },
        switch: { id, on, onClick: toggle },
      }}
    &gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/SlotContext&gt;
  )
}
// consumer
&lt;Toggle&gt;
  &lt;Label&gt;Party mode&lt;/Label&gt;
  &lt;Switch /&gt;
  &lt;Text slot=&quot;onText&quot;&gt;Let&#039;s party 🥳&lt;/Text&gt;
  &lt;Text slot=&quot;offText&quot;&gt;Sad town 😭&lt;/Text&gt;
&lt;/Toggle&gt;
Render trace

Toggle() runs, computes on/id/toggle (as before), but now builds
a single SlotContext value with four named entries — label,
onText, offText, switch — replacing what used to be a separate
ToggleContext plus three dedicated components. ToggleContext and
useToggle() no longer exist in this step.
&lt;Text slot=&quot;onText&quot;&gt; renders. useSlotProps reads props.slot (&#039;onText&#039;,
explicitly passed by the consumer this time — not a hardcoded default),
looks up slots.onText = { hidden: on ? undefined : true }, and merges
it in. Same &lt;Text&gt; component, invoked again with slot=&quot;offText&quot;,
resolves to the other entry in the same slots object.
When on flips (via &lt;Switch&gt;’s onClick={toggle} calling setOn),
Toggle re-renders, rebuilding the slots object with new hidden
values for onText/offText. Both &lt;Text&gt; instances re-render (new
SlotContext value) and each picks up its own updated hidden prop from
the same lookup as step 2 — one becomes visible, the other hides.
&lt;Switch /&gt; (no slot prop passed — falls back to its hardcoded default
&#039;switch&#039;) resolves slots.switch = { id, on, onClick } and renders the
real toggle control with the current on/toggle wired directly from
the slot lookup — no separate useToggle() hook call needed anymore.

Why
Nothing about what re-renders changes from the generic step — Toggle
flipping on still re-renders the same subtree either way. What changes is
which components exist at all: ToggleOn, ToggleOff, ToggleButton,
ToggleContext, and useToggle() all disappear, replaced by generic
Text/Switch plus data (the slots object). The slot prop is what lets
one component definition serve two different roles (onText vs offText)
simply by which string the consumer passes — the cost, per the workshop
solution’s own note, is that nothing catches a typo’d slot=&quot;onTxt&quot; at
compile time; it just silently receives no extra props.
