Prop Collections and Getters — Render Trace (Exercise 5)

Line-by-line render/execution trace through the 2 solution steps of
exercise 5 (“Prop Collections and Getters”) of EpicWeb’s
Advanced React Patterns
workshop by Kent C. Dodds. For the concept explanation (not the trace)
see State Collections and Prop Getters — this note is about
invocation order: what a click actually calls, in what sequence, and
why the getter version calls two functions where the collection version
can only ever call one.

collections — Prop Collections
Problem: every consumer of useToggle has to remember to wire both
aria-checked and onClick onto whatever element they render, correctly,
every time.
export function useToggle() {
  const [on, setOn] = useState(false)
  const toggle = () =&gt; setOn(!on)

  return {
    on,
    toggle,
    togglerProps: {
      &#039;aria-checked&#039;: on,
      onClick: toggle,
    },
  }
}

export function App() {
  const { on, togglerProps } = useToggle()
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Switch on={on} {...togglerProps} /&gt;
      &lt;hr /&gt;
      &lt;button aria-label=&quot;custom-button&quot; {...togglerProps}&gt;
        {on ? &#039;on&#039; : &#039;off&#039;}
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

App() calls useToggle(). Inside it, useState(false) returns
on = false; togglerProps is built fresh on this render as a plain
object literal: { &#039;aria-checked&#039;: false, onClick: toggle }.
App spreads togglerProps onto both &lt;Switch&gt; and the raw &lt;button&gt;.
Both elements now have an onClick prop that’s the exact same toggle
function reference, and an aria-checked reflecting the current on.
User clicks the &lt;button&gt;. React calls its onClick — which is
toggle — directly. toggle() runs setOn(!on), i.e. setOn(true).
React schedules a re-render of App (state lives in useToggle’s
useState, called from within App’s render, so App re-renders).
App() runs again. useToggle() runs again: on is now true,
togglerProps is rebuilt as a new object ({ &#039;aria-checked&#039;: true, onClick: toggle })
— a fresh literal every render, though toggle itself is a fresh closure
each render too here (it’s not wrapped in useCallback).
Both elements re-render with aria-checked=&quot;true&quot; and the updated
(recreated) onClick.

Why
Nothing here is broken yet — a single onClick source (the hook’s own
toggle) spread onto each element works fine as long as nobody needs to
add their own handler. The trace matters for what happens next: because
togglerProps.onClick is just one static function reference sitting on a
plain object, there is no mechanism by which a second handler placed next to
{...togglerProps} could combine with it — only replace it, depending on
JSX spread order.
getters — Prop Getters
Problem: a consumer wants their own onClick (e.g. logging) on the
button in addition to the toggle behavior.
function callAll&lt;Args extends Array&lt;unknown&gt;&gt;(
  ...fns: Array&lt;((...args: Args) =&gt; unknown) | undefined&gt;
) {
  return (...args: Args) =&gt; fns.forEach((fn) =&gt; fn?.(...args))
}

export function useToggle() {
  const [on, setOn] = useState(false)
  const toggle = () =&gt; setOn(!on)

  function getTogglerProps&lt;Props&gt;({
    onClick,
    ...props
  }: { onClick?: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;button&#039;&gt;[&#039;onClick&#039;] } &amp; Props) {
    return {
      &#039;aria-checked&#039;: on,
      onClick: callAll(onClick, toggle),
      ...props,
    }
  }

  return { on, toggle, getTogglerProps }
}

export function App() {
  const { on, getTogglerProps } = useToggle()
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Switch {...getTogglerProps({ on })} /&gt;
      &lt;hr /&gt;
      &lt;button
        {...getTogglerProps({
          &#039;aria-label&#039;: &#039;custom-button&#039;,
          onClick: () =&gt; console.info(&#039;onButtonClick&#039;),
          id: &#039;custom-button-id&#039;,
        })}
      &gt;
        {on ? &#039;on&#039; : &#039;off&#039;}
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

App() calls useToggle(). This render, getTogglerProps is a closure
over the current on and toggle — a fresh function each render, same
as before, but now it’s called, not spread directly.
For the &lt;button&gt;, App calls
getTogglerProps({ &#039;aria-label&#039;: &#039;custom-button&#039;, onClick: () =&gt; console.info(&#039;onButtonClick&#039;), id: &#039;custom-button-id&#039; })
during render, synchronously, before JSX for &lt;button&gt; is built.
Inside getTogglerProps, the argument is destructured:
onClick (the consumer’s log function) is pulled out; props is
everything else (&#039;aria-label&#039;, id).
callAll(onClick, toggle) is evaluated right here — it returns a
brand-new function that, when called, will invoke the consumer’s
onClick first, then toggle, in that literal argument order.
getTogglerProps returns
{ &#039;aria-checked&#039;: on, onClick: &lt;the callAll-composed function&gt;, &#039;aria-label&#039;: &#039;custom-button&#039;, id: &#039;custom-button-id&#039; }
— one merged object with exactly one onClick, spread onto &lt;button&gt;.
User clicks the button. React calls the single onClick it has — the
callAll-composed one. Inside it: fns.forEach(fn =&gt; fn?.(...args))
runs the consumer’s () =&gt; console.info(&#039;onButtonClick&#039;) first (logs to
console), then runs toggle() second (calls setOn(!on)).
React schedules a re-render off that setOn call. App re-renders,
useToggle() reruns, getTogglerProps is rebuilt with the new on, and
both the log handler and the toggle behavior are wired again identically
on the next click.

Why
The critical difference from the collections trace is step 4: merging
now happens inside the hook, at the moment getTogglerProps is called,
before React ever sees a final onClick prop. By the time JSX spreads the
result onto &lt;button&gt;, there is only ever one onClick function to spread
— so there’s no spread-order race to lose. The consumer’s behavior and the
hook’s own behavior are both guaranteed to run, every click, in the order
callAll was given them (consumer first, hook second, as called in
getTogglerProps) — a guarantee a static prop-collection object structurally
cannot make.
