State Initializers — Render Trace (Exercise 6)

Line-by-line render/execution trace through the 2 solution steps of
exercise 6 (“State Initializer”) of EpicWeb’s
Advanced React Patterns
workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Same underlying useToggle hook, evolving from
a hardcoded starting state to a consumer-configurable, reset-stable one.
For the concept explanation see State Initializers — this note is
about invocation order: which render’s initialOn a given reset()
call actually uses.

initial — Initialize Toggle
Problem: useToggle hardcodes its starting state ({ on: false }) and
has no reset capability at all — the consumer app has to fake a no-op
reset function.
type ToggleState = { on: boolean }
type ToggleAction =
  | { type: &#039;toggle&#039; }
  | { type: &#039;reset&#039;; initialState: ToggleState }

function toggleReducer(state: ToggleState, action: ToggleAction) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case &#039;toggle&#039;:
      return { on: !state.on }
    case &#039;reset&#039;:
      return action.initialState
  }
}

export function useToggle({ initialOn = false } = {}) {
  const initialState = { on: initialOn }
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(toggleReducer, initialState)
  const { on } = state

  const toggle = () =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;toggle&#039; })
  const reset = () =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;reset&#039;, initialState })

  function getTogglerProps&lt;Props&gt;({ onClick, ...props }: { onClick?: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;button&#039;&gt;[&#039;onClick&#039;] } &amp; Props) {
    return { &#039;aria-checked&#039;: on, onClick: callAll(onClick, toggle), ...props }
  }

  return { on, reset, toggle, getTogglerProps }
}

// consumer
export function App() {
  const { on, getTogglerProps, reset } = useToggle({ initialOn: true })
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Switch {...getTogglerProps({ on })} /&gt;
      &lt;hr /&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={reset}&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

App() mounts, calls useToggle({ initialOn: true }).
Inside useToggle, initialState = { on: true } is computed — a plain
object literal, created fresh on this call.
useReducer(toggleReducer, initialState) runs. React uses
initialState only on this first call to seed state; it will
never re-read this second argument again for the lifetime of this
component instance, exactly like useState’s initial argument.
toggle and reset are created as new closures on this render, each
capturing this render’s initialState binding (currently { on: true },
the same object useReducer was just seeded with).
User clicks the switch → toggle() → dispatch({ type: &#039;toggle&#039; }) →
toggleReducer returns { on: !state.on } → on becomes false.
App re-renders. Step 2 runs again: a new initialState = { on: true }
object is computed (same value, different object identity) — harmless
here because useReducer ignores it after mount.
User clicks Reset → reset() (this render’s closure) →
dispatch({ type: &#039;reset&#039;, initialState }) where initialState is
this render’s freshly-recomputed { on: true } → toggleReducer
returns action.initialState → on becomes true again. Works, by
luck: initialOn itself never changed across these renders, so every
render’s recomputed initialState happens to hold the same value.

Why
Nothing forces initialOn to stay constant across renders — it’s an
ordinary prop/argument. This step’s implementation only happens to work
because the demo never changes it after mount. Step 2 (“stability”) exposes
exactly the case where that assumption breaks.
stability — Stability
Problem: the consumer now has its own state controlling initialOn
(a button that flips it), and clicking Reset after flipping it resets to the
current initialOn, not the value the toggle actually started with.
// consumer app — initialOn is itself React state
export function App() {
  const [initialOn, setInitialOn] = useState(true)
  const { on, getTogglerProps, reset } = useToggle({ initialOn })
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setInitialOn((o) =&gt; !o)}&gt;
        initialOn is: {initialOn ? &#039;true&#039; : &#039;false&#039;}
      &lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;Switch {...getTogglerProps({ on })} /&gt;
      &lt;hr /&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={reset}&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

// buggy useToggle — unchanged from step &quot;initial&quot;
export function useToggle({ initialOn = false } = {}) {
  const initialState = { on: initialOn } // recomputed every render
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(toggleReducer, initialState)
  const reset = () =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;reset&#039;, initialState }) // this render&#039;s value
  // ...
}
Render trace — the bug

Mount: App renders with initialOn = true (its own useState).
useToggle({ initialOn: true }) seeds state = { on: true } via
useReducer, as in step “initial”.
User clicks “initialOn is: true” → setInitialOn(o =&gt; !o) → App
re-renders with initialOn = false.
useToggle({ initialOn: false }) runs again. initialState = { on: false }
is recomputed this render — a fresh object, holding the current
initialOn, not the one from mount. useReducer still ignores this
(toggle’s own on state is unaffected — it’s still whatever the user
left it at), but reset’s closure, created on this render, now
captures initialState = { on: false }.
User clicks Reset → reset() (the closure from step 3) →
dispatch({ type: &#039;reset&#039;, initialState: { on: false } }) →
toggleReducer returns { on: false }. The toggle resets to false,
even though it originally mounted on: true — the bug the README
describes: “resets to the current initialOn state, not the original
one.”

Render trace — the fix
export function useToggle({ initialOn = false } = {}) {
  const { current: initialState } = useRef&lt;ToggleState&gt;({ on: initialOn })
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(toggleReducer, initialState)
  const { on } = state
  const toggle = () =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;toggle&#039; })
  const reset = () =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;reset&#039;, initialState })
  // ...
}

Mount: useRef&lt;ToggleState&gt;({ on: initialOn }) runs with
initialOn = true. React allocates the ref object and sets
ref.current = { on: true } once. initialState is destructured
from ref.current on this render — currently { on: true }.
useReducer(toggleReducer, initialState) seeds state = { on: true },
same as before.
initialOn prop changes to false on a later render. useRef({ on: initialOn })
runs again, but per React’s contract for useRef, the argument is only
used on the very first call — ref.current is not overwritten with
{ on: false }. initialState destructured this render is still the
original ref.current object, { on: true }.
User clicks Reset → reset() (this render’s closure, but capturing the
ref-backed initialState) → dispatch({ type: &#039;reset&#039;, initialState })
where initialState is the mount-time { on: true } → toggle
correctly resets to true, regardless of what initialOn has become
since.

Why
The bug is a lifetime mismatch: reset needs a value that’s pinned to
mount time, but a plain const in the hook body is re-created on every
render, so any closure over it silently tracks whichever render created that
closure — normally invisible, but exposed the moment initialOn itself
becomes dynamic. useRef is the primitive built exactly for “give me a box
whose contents survive re-renders without being reset by them”; seeding it
once and reading .current thereafter is what makes reset refer to the
original value instead of a recent one.
Sources

EpicWeb — Advanced React Patterns workshop, exercise 6 (steps “initial” and “stability” + solutions)
react.dev — useRef

Custom Components

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.


“Components are functions which accept an object called ‘props’ and return something that is renderable.” — Kent C. Dodds

That’s the entire definition. Nothing else is special about a component. This exercise builds up to that definition iteratively, in four steps, to show exactly how JSX and createElement treat a function differently from a string.
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What is a React component, really?
A: At its core, any plain JavaScript function that accepts a single object argument (props) and returns something renderable (JSX, a string, null, an array, etc.) qualifies as a component — there’s no class requirement, no special syntax, no inheritance. React’s only real requirement is that it controls when that function gets called, not you. In our example, message/Message is exactly this: a function taking props and returning a &lt;div&gt;.
Q: What’s the difference between calling a function directly versus passing it to createElement (or using it in JSX)?
A: This distinction matters for every component, not just this example: calling a function directly (message({...})) is an ordinary JS call — React never receives a reference to it, so it can’t decide when to invoke it, skip re-invoking it, or later attach hooks/state to it. Handing the function itself to createElement/JSX instead gives React that reference, so React owns the scheduling of the call — which is the mechanism that makes hooks, reconciliation, and lazy rendering possible at all. In our example, message({ children: &#039;Hello World&#039; }) runs immediately as a plain call, while createElement(message, { children: &#039;Hello World&#039; }) lets React decide when message actually executes.
message({ children: &#039;Hi&#039; })              // plain call — React has no say in this
createElement(message, { children: &#039;Hi&#039; }) // reference — React controls invocation
Q: Why must custom component names be capitalized in JSX?
A: This is a JSX/Babel compilation rule that applies to any component, anywhere: Babel decides whether a tag name compiles to a string (createElement(&#039;div&#039;, ...)) or a variable reference (createElement(Message, ...)) purely from the tag’s casing — lowercase/kebab-case tags always become strings meant to match native HTML elements, while capitalized (or property-access) tags become references to your component. In our example, renaming message to Message and writing &lt;Message&gt; (instead of &lt;message&gt;) is what makes Babel treat it as a component reference rather than an attempt to render a nonexistent &lt;message&gt; HTML element.
Q: Is there anything special about the children prop?
A: No — across all of React, children is just a regular prop that JSX gives special syntax sugar for nesting content between tags; under the hood it’s passed exactly like any other prop. A component can define and accept any prop names, primitives, objects, or other elements — children isn’t reserved or magic. In our example, &lt;Message&gt;Hello World&lt;/Message&gt; compiles to the same thing as &lt;Message children=&quot;Hello World&quot; /&gt;.
Q: Does a component’s function body persist state between renders?
A: No, and this is universal to all function components: React re-invokes the entire function from scratch on every render, so any plain local variable (let, const) is reinitialized every time and can’t “remember” anything across renders on its own. This is precisely the problem hooks like useState/useRef exist to solve — they let React persist a value across a function’s re-invocations by tracking it outside the function body. In our example, a let declared inside Calculator would reset on every render; nothing in this exercise persists state yet since hooks haven’t been introduced.
Simple Function

Lesson

function message(props) {
  return &lt;div className=&quot;message&quot;&gt;{props.children}&lt;/div&gt;
}

const element = (
  &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;
    {message({ children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })}
    {message({ children: &#039;Goodbye World&#039; })}
  &lt;/div&gt;
)

💬 This reduces duplication, but it is not a real React component yet — it’s just a plain function being called directly and interpolated. The distinction matters: React never gets a reference to message, so it can’t defer calling it, track it, or later attach state/effects to it. That’s the gap the next two steps close.

Raw API

Lesson

const element = &lt;div&gt;{createElement(message, { children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })}&lt;/div&gt;

💬 Passing message itself (not calling it) as the first argument to createElement is the semantic shift: React now owns calling this function, and defers doing so until it’s actually necessary to render. Console-logging inside message before and after this change shows the calls happening in a different order — proof that React, not your code, now controls when the function runs. Children passed this way show up as props.children (as an array, if there’s more than one).

JSX Components

Lesson

function Message(props) {
  return &lt;div className=&quot;message&quot;&gt;{props.children}&lt;/div&gt;
}

const element = &lt;Message&gt;Hello World&lt;/Message&gt;

💬 Babel decides whether JSX tag names compile to a string (createElement(&#039;message&#039;, ...)) or a reference (createElement(message, ...)) purely from the tag’s casing/shape: capitalized (&lt;Capitalized&gt;) and property-access (&lt;foo.Bar&gt;) tags compile to references; lowercase and kebab-case tags compile to strings. Since there’s no native &lt;message&gt; DOM element, &lt;message&gt; would throw a “did you mean to render a component?” warning. Convention: capitalize custom component names.

Props

Lesson

function Calculator({ left, operator, right }) {
  const result = operations[operator](left, right)
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;code&gt;
        {left} {operator} {right} = &lt;output&gt;{result}&lt;/output&gt;
      &lt;/code&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

// &lt;Calculator left={1} operator=&quot;+&quot; right={2} /&gt;

💬 Props are entirely up to the component author — there’s nothing special about children beyond JSX’s implicit syntax for it (&lt;Message&gt;x&lt;/Message&gt; ≡ &lt;Message children=&quot;x&quot; /&gt;). A component can accept any prop names, primitives, objects, or other React elements. Think of a component as a function and JSX usage as a function call — React just controls when that “call” actually happens.


💬 One implication worth internalizing early: React re-invokes a component’s function on every render, from scratch — it isn’t called once and kept alive. A let variable declared inside Calculator gets reset to its initial value every single time. Nothing persists between calls yet, which is exactly the gap useState (from the React Hooks workshop) exists to fill. Also, because a component is just a value, you can choose one dynamically like any other variable: const Panel = isAdmin ? AdminPanel : UserPanel then &lt;Panel /&gt; — no special API needed for that. (In real codebases, the PascalCase convention itself is usually enforced by eslint-plugin-react’s naming rules rather than left to memory.)

Error Boundaries

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

An unhandled error during render removes your entire app from the page, leaving a blank screen. You can’t just wrap JSX in try/catch because — as seen in Custom Components — JSX only describes elements; the component functions aren’t actually called until React gets around to rendering them, at a point your try/catch has already exited. The fix is a special component category called an Error Boundary, used from the third-party react-error-boundary package (Kent is a maintainer) rather than hand-rolled, since Error Boundaries can currently only be implemented as class components.
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why can’t you just use try/catch around JSX to handle render errors?
A: This is true of any framework built on a virtual DOM, not just this example: JSX only describes elements as data — the actual component functions aren’t called until React later walks that tree during its own render pass, well after your try/catch block has already exited. Error Boundaries exist specifically as a React-level mechanism to catch errors thrown during React’s own render process, which a JS-level try/catch structurally can’t reach. In our example, wrapping &lt;OnboardingForm /&gt; in JSX with a surrounding try/catch wouldn’t catch anything, because OnboardingForm isn’t actually invoked until React renders it.
Q: Why does the component that might throw need to be a child of the ErrorBoundary, not merged into the same component?
A: This is a general rule for any error-boundary implementation, in any UI framework that has the concept: a boundary can only catch errors from components strictly beneath it in the tree — it can’t catch an error that occurs while React is still constructing the boundary’s own surrounding tree, since the boundary hasn’t been “seen” yet at that point. In our example, that’s why OnboardingForm (the risky component) sits as a JSX child of &lt;ErrorBoundary&gt; inside App, rather than the error-throwing logic being inlined into App itself:
&lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}&gt;
  &lt;OnboardingForm /&gt;
&lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
Q: What kinds of errors can an Error Boundary NOT catch automatically?
A: Universally, React’s built-in error catching only covers synchronous errors thrown during rendering — anything outside that call stack (event handlers, useEffect callbacks, rejected promises, timers) is invisible to a boundary by default, along with errors the boundary component itself throws and errors thrown while rendering the fallback UI. In our example, an error thrown inside handleSubmit (an event handler) needs to be manually forwarded with useErrorBoundary()’s showBoundary(error):
const { showBoundary } = useErrorBoundary()
try {
  // risky work in an event handler
} catch (error) {
  showBoundary(error)
}
Q: What does resetErrorBoundary actually do?
A: Generically, it’s the boundary’s built-in recovery mechanism: calling it clears the caught error and re-mounts whatever the boundary wraps, as if rendering for the first time — a re-mount, not a restore, so any local state the wrapped component held is genuinely gone unless it was persisted outside the component beforehand. In our example, the fallback’s “Try again” button calls resetErrorBoundary to re-mount OnboardingForm fresh:
function ErrorFallback({ error, resetErrorBoundary }: FallbackProps) {
  return &lt;button onClick={resetErrorBoundary}&gt;Try again&lt;/button&gt;
}
Composition

Lesson

import { ErrorBoundary, type FallbackProps } from &#039;react-error-boundary&#039;

function ErrorFallback({ error }: FallbackProps) {
  return &lt;div role=&quot;alert&quot;&gt;Something went wrong: {error.message}&lt;/div&gt;
}

function App() {
  return (
    &lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}&gt;
      &lt;OnboardingForm /&gt;
    &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
  )
}

💬 The component that might throw (OnboardingForm, renamed from App) has to be a child of the ErrorBoundary, not fused into the same component — if the error happens while React is still in the process of creating the App element tree, the ErrorBoundary hasn’t been “seen” by React yet, so it can’t catch anything. Splitting into an outer App (owns the ErrorBoundary) and an inner form component is what makes the boundary effective. This mirrors try/catch: an Error Boundary only catches errors from components it wraps, and you can nest multiple boundaries for more localized error UI (e.g. separate boundaries around a list and a detail view, so an error in one doesn’t take down the other).

Other Errors

Lesson

import { useErrorBoundary } from &#039;react-error-boundary&#039;

function OnboardingForm() {
  const { showBoundary } = useErrorBoundary()

  function handleSubmit(formData: FormData) {
    try {
      // ... something that might throw
    } catch (error) {
      showBoundary(error)
    }
  }
  // ...
}

💬 React’s built-in error catching only covers errors thrown during rendering. Errors thrown from event handlers, useEffect callbacks, or a rejected promise happen outside React’s own call stack, so an Error Boundary can’t see them automatically — same reason a try/catch around a synchronous call can’t catch an error from an unrelated later callback. useErrorBoundary()’s showBoundary(error) is the manual bridge: catch the error yourself (try/catch, or a promise’s second .then argument / .catch), then hand it to the nearest Error Boundary explicitly.


💬 The complete list of what an Error Boundary can’t catch on its own: event handlers and async code (just covered above), errors thrown by the boundary component itself (a boundary can’t catch its own errors — only a boundary further up the tree can), and errors thrown while rendering the fallback UI (that also needs a boundary above it, or it takes down the tree the same as any other uncaught error). React 19 also added root-level onCaughtError/onUncaughtError options (passed to createRoot) for centralized error logging that runs alongside whatever Error Boundaries you have in place — useful for reporting even the errors a boundary does catch, since a caught error still shouldn’t go unlogged.

Reset

Lesson

function ErrorFallback({ error, resetErrorBoundary }: FallbackProps) {
  return (
    &lt;div role=&quot;alert&quot;&gt;
      Something went wrong: {error.message}
      &lt;button onClick={resetErrorBoundary}&gt;Try again&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

💬 resetErrorBoundary (passed to the fallback component alongside error) clears the error and re-mounts whatever the boundary wraps — as if it were rendering for the first time. Note “re-mount,” not “restore”: any local state the wrapped component held (typed-in form values, etc.) is genuinely gone, not preserved. If preserving that state matters, it has to be persisted somewhere outside the component (lifted state, local storage) before the reset happens.

Forms

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

React doesn’t add much on top of what forms already do natively — most of this exercise is really about the web platform’s form APIs (onSubmit, FormData, action, method, encType), plus React’s own convenience layer on top (a function-valued action prop).
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why default to method=&quot;POST&quot; instead of leaving a form on the default GET?
A: This is a plain HTML/HTTP concern, true of any form on any site: with GET, submitted field values — including passwords — get serialized into the destination URL as a query string, which then lingers in browser history, server access logs, and any analytics that capture URLs. POST moves the data into the request body instead, which isn’t logged or bookmarked the same way. In our example, the onboarding form is switched from the default GET to method=&quot;POST&quot; for exactly this reason.
&lt;form action=&quot;api/onboarding&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;&lt;!-- ... --&gt;&lt;/form&gt;
Q: Why do file uploads need encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot;?
A: Also a general HTML rule: the default body encoding, application/x-www-form-urlencoded, can only serialize simple text key/value pairs, so a &lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt; field gets reduced to just its filename string instead of the actual binary content. multipart/form-data encodes each field as a separate part of the request body, which is what allows real file bytes to be transmitted. In our example, the onboarding form’s photo upload only works once encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot; is added alongside method=&quot;POST&quot;.
Q: What does passing a function to a form’s action prop actually do, and why is that React-specific?
A: This is a React-only capability, not standard HTML — HTML attributes can only ever hold strings, so a function-valued action requires a framework to intercept it. When you pass a function, React calls it with a ready-made FormData object, automatically prevents the default full-page-refresh submission, and resets the form’s fields afterward — replacing the manual onSubmit + event.preventDefault() + new FormData(event.currentTarget) dance every form used to need. In our example, handleOnboarding receives the submitted FormData directly with none of that boilerplate:
function handleOnboarding(formData: FormData) {
  console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
}

&lt;form action={handleOnboarding}&gt;{/* ... */}&lt;/form&gt;
Q: How do useActionState and useFormStatus relate to form actions?
A: Both are React 19 hooks that build on the same function-action mechanism generally: useActionState wraps any action to track its pending/result/error state across submissions (so you don’t manage that state by hand), and useFormStatus lets a descendant of the &lt;form&gt; — like a submit button — read whether any action on that form is currently pending, without prop-drilling. This fundamentals exercise doesn’t implement either (the onboarding form here has no pending/error UI), but they’re the natural next step once you need to show submission feedback — see useFormStatus for a full example of useFormStatus in practice.
Form

Lesson

&lt;form&gt;
  &lt;label htmlFor=&quot;usernameInput&quot;&gt;Username&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input id=&quot;usernameInput&quot; name=&quot;username&quot; type=&quot;text&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;

💬 Associating a &lt;label&gt; with its input (via matching htmlFor/id, or by nesting the input inside the label) isn’t cosmetic — without it, screen readers announce the input with no name at all. Explicitly setting type=&quot;submit&quot; (or type=&quot;button&quot;) on every button is worth the extra prop: an un-typed &lt;button&gt; defaults to submit, so a decorative button nested inside a form you didn’t expect it to interact with can accidentally submit that form.

Form Action

Lesson

&lt;form action=&quot;api/onboarding&quot;&gt;&lt;!-- ... --&gt;&lt;/form&gt;

💬 By default, a form submits to the current page’s URL. The action attribute (a genuine HTML feature, not React-specific) controls the destination. With the default GET method, submitted values get serialized into that destination’s URL as a query string.

Input Types

Lesson

&lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; name=&quot;password&quot; /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;file&quot; name=&quot;photo&quot; accept=&quot;image/*&quot; /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;color&quot; name=&quot;favoriteColor&quot; /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;date&quot; name=&quot;startDate&quot; /&gt;

💬 The full list of standard input types covers a lot of built-in behavior for free (password masks input and is excluded from back/forward-cache restoration for security; file + accept restricts the file picker). Native inputs like color and date cover MVP needs but are hard to restyle — for a fully custom look, reach for a library like Radix UI or React Aria Components.

Submission

Lesson

&lt;form
  action=&quot;api/onboarding&quot;
  method=&quot;POST&quot;
  encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot;
  onSubmit={(event) =&gt; {
    event.preventDefault()
    const formData = new FormData(event.currentTarget)
    console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
  }}
&gt;
  {/* ... */}
&lt;/form&gt;

💬 Three separate problems, three separate fixes: (1) GET puts every field — including passwords — into the URL, which ends up in browser history and server logs, so switch to method=&quot;POST&quot; to move data into the request body; (2) the default body encoding (application/x-www-form-urlencoded) can only serialize a file as its filename string, so encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot; is required for real file uploads; (3) neither of those stops the browser’s full-page refresh on submit — only event.preventDefault() inside onSubmit does that. event.currentTarget gives you the &lt;form&gt; DOM node, which the FormData constructor reads directly.

Form Actions

Lesson

function handleOnboarding(formData: FormData) {
  console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
}

&lt;form action={handleOnboarding}&gt;{/* ... */}&lt;/form&gt;

💬 The action prop accepting a function is a React-specific feature — HTML attributes can’t hold function values. Passing a function here replaces the onSubmit + preventDefault + manual FormData construction from Submission entirely: React calls the function with a ready-made FormData object, handles preventing the default browser behavior, and resets the form’s fields back to their defaults after the function runs.


💬 This exercise covers the foundation, but a real onboarding form usually also needs to show a pending state while the action runs and surface a validation error afterward. React 19 builds two hooks directly on top of this same action mechanism for exactly that: useActionState wraps an action to track its pending/result/error state across submissions, and useFormStatus lets a descendant of the &lt;form&gt; (like a submit button) read whether any action on that form is currently pending, without prop-drilling it down. Out of scope for this fundamentals workshop, but the natural next step once you’re building real forms.

Inputs

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Form elements mostly work the same in React as in HTML, with a handful of React-specific exceptions — especially around default values, which this exercise walks through one input type at a time.
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What’s the difference between value and defaultValue on an input?
A: This is React’s general controlled-vs-uncontrolled distinction, applicable to every input type: setting value makes the input “controlled” — React pins it to exactly that value on every render, so the user can’t type into it at all unless you also wire up onChange to update that value yourself. defaultValue (a React-only prop, not in the HTML spec) sets only the initial value, after which the DOM manages the input’s state and the user can freely edit it — the right choice whenever you want “pre-fill but stay editable.” In our example, every input in the “Default Value” section uses defaultValue/defaultChecked (never value) precisely so users can still type over the pre-filled data:
&lt;input type=&quot;number&quot; defaultValue={18} /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; defaultChecked /&gt;
Q: How does an unchecked checkbox or empty radio group show up in submitted FormData?
A: This is standard web-platform behavior for any HTML form, not something React introduces: an unchecked checkbox or a radio group with nothing selected simply has its field absent from FormData entirely — never false, never an empty string, just missing — while a checked checkbox submits the literal string &quot;on&quot; unless you give it an explicit value. In our example, the waiverSigned checkbox and the visibility radio group both follow this rule, so checking for the field’s presence (not its truthiness) is how you’d read them server-side.
Q: Why can’t you set a value/defaultValue on a file input?
A: Browsers block this universally, in every framework, as a security measure — no code should be able to hand a user a pre-selected file, since that could be used to exfiltrate files the user never explicitly chose. To reset a file input back to empty, you need an imperative escape hatch: ref.current.value = &#039;&#039;, or forcing React to remount the input by changing its key. In our example, there’s no value/defaultValue at all on the file input in the “Hidden Inputs”/photo-upload cases — resetting it would require one of those two workarounds.
Q: Why does a &lt;select&gt; always have a value on submission even if the user never touches it, while a radio group doesn’t?
A: This follows directly from how each element is specified in HTML: a &lt;select&gt; always has some &lt;option&gt; selected — whichever one appears first by default — so it always contributes a value to FormData, whereas a radio group has no such forced default and is simply absent if nothing’s checked. In our example, that’s why the accountType select lists an explicit &lt;option value=&quot;&quot;&gt;Please select an option&lt;/option&gt; first — without it, &quot;admin&quot; (the next option) would be silently pre-selected and submitted even if the user never opened the dropdown.
&lt;select name=&quot;accountType&quot;&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;&quot;&gt;Please select an option&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;admin&quot;&gt;Admin&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;
Checkbox

Lesson

&lt;label&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; name=&quot;waiverSigned&quot; /&gt; I have signed the waiver
&lt;/label&gt;

💬 Checkboxes are commonly nested inside their label (rather than using htmlFor/id) — clicking the label text still toggles the box. The quirky part is how a checkbox is represented in submitted FormData: checked → the field appears with the literal string value &quot;on&quot;; unchecked → the field is absent entirely (not false, not present at all). This is a long-standing web-platform behavior, not something React introduces.

Select

Lesson

&lt;select id=&quot;accountType&quot; name=&quot;accountType&quot;&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;&quot;&gt;Please select an option&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;admin&quot;&gt;Admin&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;teacher&quot;&gt;Teacher&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;parent&quot;&gt;Parent&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;student&quot;&gt;Student&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;

💬 &lt;select&gt; is a compound element — the label is on the &lt;select&gt; itself, but each &lt;option&gt;’s visible text is separate from its submitted value. Unlike an unset checkbox or radio group, a &lt;select&gt; always has a value on submission: whichever &lt;option&gt; appears first is selected by default, so an explicit “please choose” placeholder option needs to be first if you don’t want a real value pre-selected.

Radios

Lesson

&lt;fieldset&gt;
  &lt;legend&gt;Visibility&lt;/legend&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;
    &lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot; name=&quot;visibility&quot; value=&quot;public&quot; /&gt; Public
  &lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;
    &lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot; name=&quot;visibility&quot; value=&quot;private&quot; /&gt; Private
  &lt;/label&gt;
&lt;/fieldset&gt;

💬 A radio group needs two layers of labeling: &lt;fieldset&gt; + &lt;legend&gt; label the group as a whole (announced to assistive tech as “what am I choosing between”), while each individual &lt;label&gt; names one option. All inputs in the group share the same name — that’s what makes them mutually exclusive. Unlike &lt;select&gt;, a radio group has no forced default: if nothing is checked, the field is simply absent from FormData on submit (same absent-when-unset behavior as an unchecked checkbox).

Hidden Inputs

Lesson

&lt;input type=&quot;hidden&quot; name=&quot;orgId&quot; value=&quot;123&quot; /&gt;

💬 A hidden input submits a value with the form without the user seeing or interacting with it (invisible on screen, and skipped by screen readers). Useful for data the client already knows and doesn’t need to ask the user for — an ID, an org context, a CSRF-style token. It can also carry a JSON string (JSON.stringify({...})) if a single field needs to represent a more complex value.

Default Value

Lesson

&lt;select defaultValue=&quot;student&quot;&gt;...&lt;/select&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;number&quot; defaultValue={18} /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;color&quot; defaultValue=&quot;#002E5D&quot; /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; defaultChecked /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot; name=&quot;visibility&quot; value=&quot;public&quot; defaultChecked /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;date&quot; defaultValue={new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10)} /&gt;

💬 This is the sharpest React-specific gotcha in forms: setting the value prop on an input makes it a “controlled” input — React will keep it pinned to exactly that value and the user cannot type into it unless you also wire up an onChange to update that value yourself. defaultValue (a React-only prop, not in the HTML spec) sets the initial value only, after which the user can freely change it — which is what you want for “pre-fill but stay editable” fields. Checkboxes and radios use the parallel defaultChecked prop instead of defaultValue. Everything gets coerced to a string, so defaultValue={18} and defaultValue=&quot;18&quot; are equivalent; a date’s defaultValue string must be YYYY-MM-DD to match the format the input actually submits.


💬 One input type is the exception to all of this: &lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt; has no value or defaultValue at all — browsers block setting one for security (you can’t hand the user a fake pre-selected file). So “resetting” a file input to empty can’t be done via props; it takes either an imperative ref.current.value = &#039;&#039;, or the key-reset trick covered later in this workshop (changing the input’s key forces React to remount it with a clean slate).

React Fundamentals — Quiz Log
A self-quiz through the React Fundamentals workshop: one question per exercise, sourced via the epicshop MCP server’s get_quiz_instructions tool (which pulls each exercise’s real problem/solution content), then answered here as a self-check rather than back-and-forth.
1. Hello World in JS
Q: You run this code and the page stays blank — no error either:
const rootElement = document.getElementById(&#039;root&#039;)
const element = document.createElement(&#039;div&#039;)
element.textContent = &#039;Hello World&#039;
Why doesn’t “Hello World” appear, and what’s the missing line?
A: document.createElement only builds the node in memory — it’s never attached to the visible document tree. Creating and mutating an element (setting textContent) doesn’t put it on the page; only appending it to something already in the document does. Fix:
const rootElement = document.getElementById(&#039;root&#039;)
const element = document.createElement(&#039;div&#039;)
element.textContent = &#039;Hello World&#039;
rootElement.append(element) // ← the missing line
2. Raw React APIs
Q: What does createElement(&#039;div&#039;, { className: &#039;container&#039;, children: &#039;Hello World&#039; }) actually return, and at what point does React touch the real DOM?
A: It returns a plain JavaScript object — a UI descriptor, not a DOM node:
const element = createElement(&#039;div&#039;, { className: &#039;container&#039;, children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })
console.log(element)
// { type: &#039;div&#039;, props: { className: &#039;container&#039;, children: &#039;Hello World&#039; }, ... }

createRoot(rootElement).render(element) // ← only NOW does React touch the real DOM
Nothing before that render call creates or modifies a single DOM node — createElement just builds a description of what should exist.
3. Using JSX
Q: Why does the browser throw Unexpected token &#039;&lt;&#039; when it hits raw JSX like &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;Hello World&lt;/div&gt; in a &lt;script&gt; tag, and what two things fix it?
A: Browsers only understand JavaScript, and JSX isn’t JavaScript — it’s HTML-like syntax the browser’s parser doesn’t recognize. It needs a compiler (Babel) to transform it into createElement calls first:
&lt;script src=&quot;/babel-standalone.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script type=&quot;text/babel&quot; data-type=&quot;module&quot;&gt;
  import * as React from &#039;/react.js&#039;
  const element = &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;Hello World&lt;/div&gt;
  // Babel compiles this to: React.createElement(&#039;div&#039;, { className: &#039;container&#039;, children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })
&lt;/script&gt;
Two things fix it: (1) type=&quot;text/babel&quot; tells Babel to transpile this script instead of the browser trying to parse it directly, and (2) import * as React puts React in scope, since the compiled output calls React.createElement(...).
4. Custom Components
Q: Given Kent’s definition — “components are functions which accept an object called props and return something renderable” — why does &lt;message&gt;Hello World&lt;/message&gt; (lowercase) fail to render your message function, while &lt;Message&gt;Hello World&lt;/Message&gt; (capitalized) works?
A: Babel decides how to compile a JSX tag purely from its casing:
function message(props) {
  return &lt;div className=&quot;message&quot;&gt;{props.children}&lt;/div&gt;
}

&lt;message&gt;Hello World&lt;/message&gt;
// compiles to: createElement(&#039;message&#039;, { children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })
// → React looks for a native DOM element called &quot;message&quot; — doesn&#039;t exist, warns/fails

&lt;Message&gt;Hello World&lt;/Message&gt;
// compiles to: createElement(Message, { children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })
// → React calls the Message function itself
A lowercase tag name compiles to a string, which React interprets as a request for a native DOM element. A capitalized tag compiles to a reference, passing the actual function so React can call it. Capitalizing components is the convention that makes this distinction visible in the code.
5. TypeScript with React
Q: Typing operator: string on CalculatorProps technically works, but why is it a bad type — and what one keyword lets operations stay the single source of truth for which operators are valid, without you having to widen or hand-list them elsewhere?
A: string accepts any string, including &#039;**&#039; — which compiles fine but throws at runtime because operations[&#039;**&#039;] doesn’t exist:
type CalculatorProps = { left: number; operator: string; right: number }
// ✅ compiles, 💥 crashes at runtime:
&lt;Calculator left={2} operator=&quot;**&quot; right={3} /&gt;
The fix is deriving the type from the operations object itself instead of hand-writing (and duplicating) a union, using satisfies so the object’s own narrow literal keys aren’t widened away:
type OperationFn = (left: number, right: number) =&gt; number

const operations = {
  &#039;+&#039;: (left: number, right: number) =&gt; left + right,
  &#039;-&#039;: (left: number, right: number) =&gt; left - right,
  &#039;*&#039;: (left: number, right: number) =&gt; left * right,
  &#039;/&#039;: (left: number, right: number) =&gt; left / right,
} satisfies Record&lt;string, OperationFn&gt;

type Operator = keyof typeof operations // &#039;+&#039; | &#039;-&#039; | &#039;*&#039; | &#039;/&#039;, derived, not duplicated
type CalculatorProps = { left: number; operator: Operator; right: number }
6. Styling
Q: In a Box component that merges a default style with a caller-provided one —
style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }}
— why does the caller’s style prop win over fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, and how would you flip that so fontStyle can never be overridden?
A: Object spread follows plain JS rules: whatever comes later in the object literal wins on key collisions.
// caller&#039;s fontStyle can override the default — spread comes last:
style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }}

// component&#039;s fontStyle always wins — spread comes first:
style={{ ...style, fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039; }}
Spreading ...style after fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039; lets any fontStyle the caller passes override the default. Flipping the order to { ...style, fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039; } makes the component’s value always win — the caller can only fill in whatever else style doesn’t already define.
7. Forms
Q: A form submission is losing full page state (annoying), leaking a password into the URL (dangerous), and uploading a file as just its filename string (broken). Three separate problems — what three separate attributes/handlers fix each one, and why doesn’t fixing one also fix the others?
A: They’re independent because each addresses a different default browser behavior:
&lt;form
  action=&quot;api/onboarding&quot;
  method=&quot;POST&quot;                      // (1) fields go in the request body, not the URL
  encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot;      // (2) lets real file bytes be sent, not just a filename
  onSubmit={(event) =&gt; {
    event.preventDefault()           // (3) stops the browser&#039;s default full-page refresh
    const formData = new FormData(event.currentTarget)
    console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
  }}
&gt;
  {/* ... */}
&lt;/form&gt;
(1) the default method=&quot;GET&quot; serializes all fields — including the password — into the URL, so method=&quot;POST&quot; moves the payload into the request body instead; (2) the default body encoding (application/x-www-form-urlencoded) can only represent a file as its filename string, so encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot; is required for real file bytes to be sent; (3) neither of those stops the browser’s default full-page refresh on submit — only event.preventDefault() does that. None of the three implies the others. (React’s function-valued action prop, covered later in the same exercise, replaces all three at once.)
8. Inputs
Q: A form has &lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; name=&quot;waiverSigned&quot; /&gt;. What ends up in the submitted FormData when the box is checked, and what happens when it’s left unchecked? Why doesn’t setting value=&quot;true&quot; and reading it as a boolean work here?
A: Checked → the field is present with the literal string &quot;on&quot;. Unchecked → the field is absent from FormData entirely — not false, not present with an empty value, just missing.
const formData = new FormData(formElement)
console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
// checkbox checked:   { waiverSigned: &#039;on&#039;, /* ...other fields */ }
// checkbox unchecked:  { /* waiverSigned key is just not there at all */ }

// so read it by presence, not by coercing a value:
const waiverSigned = formData.has(&#039;waiverSigned&#039;)
This is a long-standing web-platform quirk (not React’s doing), which is why you check for the field’s presence rather than trying to coerce its value to a boolean.
9. Error Boundaries
Q: Why can’t you wrap your top-level App component’s own JSX in an &lt;ErrorBoundary&gt; if the error happens while App’s elements are being created — and what’s the structural fix the workshop uses?
A: JSX only describes elements via createElement calls; a component function isn’t actually invoked until React later processes that description. If the error is thrown while App itself is being turned into elements, React hasn’t “seen” any ErrorBoundary yet — the boundary and the failure are in the same unfinished tree.
// ❌ doesn&#039;t work — if OnboardingForm&#039;s own body throws while App renders,
// there&#039;s no boundary wrapping it yet at that point:
function App() {
  return (
    &lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}&gt;
      {/* ...App&#039;s own JSX that might itself throw... */}
    &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
  )
}

// ✅ works — split into two components so the risky one is a genuine child:
function OnboardingForm() {
  /* the component that might throw */
}

function App() {
  return (
    &lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}&gt;
      &lt;OnboardingForm /&gt;
    &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
  )
}
The fix: rename the original component to something like OnboardingForm, and make a new outer App that renders &lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;&lt;OnboardingForm /&gt;&lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;. Now the risky component is a genuine child the boundary can catch errors from.
10. Rendering Arrays
Q: Given items.map(item =&gt; &lt;li&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;), why does React warn about a missing key, and specifically why is items.map((item, index) =&gt; &lt;li key={index}&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;) usually not a real fix?
A: When an array of elements re-renders after a change, React can’t tell how it changed — a reordered array and an array whose items all got new text look structurally identical without extra information.
// ❌ no identity information — React guesses from position:
{items.map((item) =&gt; &lt;li&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;)}

// ❌ index &quot;key&quot; is still just position — same problem once items reorder/get removed:
{items.map((item, index) =&gt; &lt;li key={index}&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;)}

// ✅ a real, stable identity — React can match old elements to new by data, not position:
{items.map((item) =&gt; &lt;li key={item.id}&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;)}
The key supplies the missing identity, so React can match old elements to new ones by the data they represent instead of guessing from position. Using the array index as the key just re-encodes “position” as the key — behaviorally identical to having no key at all once the array is reordered or an item is removed from the middle, so it fails to fix exactly the bug it’s meant to solve. The key needs to come from the item’s own stable identity (a real ID).

Rendering Arrays

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Rendering a hard-coded list of elements is unambiguous to React — but interpolating an array of elements (list.map(item =&gt; &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;)) removes information React needs to track changes over time, which is why React warns about a missing key prop whenever you render an array.
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why does React need a key prop when rendering arrays?
A: Generally, React’s reconciliation algorithm diffs the previous render’s element tree against the new one to decide what DOM work is actually needed, and for a list it needs some way to match “this element in the old tree” to “this element in the new tree” — key is that stable identity. Without it, React falls back to matching by position, which looks identical whether an item was added at the start or every item’s content shifted down by one — and that ambiguity breaks anything with state tied to a specific DOM node (focus, input values, text selection, animations). In our example, giving each &lt;li&gt; key={item.id} lets React correctly track each list item across re-renders instead of guessing from position.
&lt;ul&gt;
  {items.map((item) =&gt; (
    &lt;li key={item.id}&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;
  ))}
&lt;/ul&gt;
Q: Why is using the array index as a key a bad idea?
A: In general, the index is position — exactly the information a key exists to replace — so whenever items are inserted, removed, or reordered, index-based keys cause React to misattribute old state (focus, inputs, component instances) to the wrong item, the same class of bug as having no key at all. In our example, this shows up as: select text in an input, delete a preceding item so the list shifts, and the selection/focus follows the position rather than the item, because the index-derived key didn’t move with the data.
Q: What are the rules for a valid key?
A: As a general rule, a key must uniquely identify a piece of data among its siblings in that one array (not globally — the same key value can be reused in an unrelated list elsewhere in the app) and must stay stable across re-renders of the same logical item. In our example, generating a key with Math.random() or Date.now() inside .map() would violate the stability rule — a fresh key every render tells React “this is a brand-new element” every time, forcing a full remount and state loss on every render.
Q: What else can changing a key be used for, besides array rendering?
A: More generally, a key isn’t only for lists — changing any element’s key, for any reason, tells React “treat this as a conceptually new element,” which makes React unmount the old instance (discarding all its internal state) and mount a fresh one. This is a recognized technique for forcibly resetting a component’s state without writing explicit “clear the fields” logic. In our example, bumping a resetKey counter and using it as the &lt;input&gt;’s key clears the input by remounting it.
const [resetKey, setResetKey] = useState(0)

&lt;input key={resetKey} /&gt;
&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setResetKey((k) =&gt; k + 1)}&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;
Key prop

Lesson

&lt;ul&gt;
  {items.map((item) =&gt; (
    &lt;li key={item.id}&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;
  ))}
&lt;/ul&gt;

💬 When an array of elements re-renders after a change, React can’t tell how it changed — an item added at the start looks identical, structurally, to every item’s content shifting down by one. Without a key, React just guesses (typically: keep elements in position, update their content) — which is fine for stateless text, but breaks badly for anything with state the DOM itself is tracking (an input’s typed value, focus, text selection). The rule: every element in a rendered array needs a key prop that uniquely identifies the data it represents — an item’s real ID, not its position.

Focus State

Lesson


💬 This step is exploratory rather than a code change: it demonstrates that using the array index as a key produces the same problems as no key at all — because the index is really just “position,” which is exactly the information a key is supposed to replace. If you select text in an input, then delete a preceding item so items shift, the selection/focus follows the position, not the item, when the key is missing or is the index. Only a key derived from the item’s own stable identity (an ID) lets React correctly track “this specific piece of UI state belongs to this specific piece of data” as the array reorders.


💬 Two more rules about keys worth internalizing: they only need to be unique among siblings within that one array — reusing the same key value across two unrelated lists elsewhere in the app is completely fine, keys aren’t a global namespace. And a key must be stable across re-renders of the same logical item — generating one with Math.random() or Date.now() inside the .map() defeats the entire purpose, since a fresh key every render tells React “this is a brand-new element” every time, forcing a full remount (and state loss) on every single render instead of preventing it.

Key Reset

Lesson

const [resetKey, setResetKey] = useState(0)

&lt;input key={resetKey} /&gt;
&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setResetKey((k) =&gt; k + 1)}&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;

💬 A key isn’t only for array reconciliation — changing an element’s key for any reason tells React “this is conceptually a new element,” so it unmounts the old instance (discarding all its internal state) and mounts a fresh one in its place. Deliberately changing a key is therefore a legitimate technique for forcibly resetting a component’s (or a DOM element’s) state, without writing any explicit “clear the fields” logic — not something reached for often, but useful to recognize.

Styling

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Two primary ways to style in React: the style prop (inline) and the className prop (CSS classes) — both are property names, not the HTML attribute names (class, not a string of raw CSS).
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: How does the style prop differ between HTML and JSX?
A: This is a general JSX convention, not just a React quirk: HTML’s inline style attribute is a single CSS string, but JSX props are JS expressions, so style takes a JS object with camelCased property names mirroring the DOM’s CSSStyleDeclaration (e.g. background-color → backgroundColor), and bare numbers get px appended automatically for most properties. In our example, style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, backgroundColor: &#039;lightblue&#039; }} is an object literal passed through a JSX expression ({}), not special syntax.
&lt;div style={{ backgroundColor: &#039;lightblue&#039;, marginTop: 20 }}&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
Q: How do you let a component’s caller override its default styles/classes?
A: This is a general “component wraps a native element” pattern in React: accept className/style as props on your component, then merge them with your own defaults so the caller’s values win — spread order controls precedence, since a later key in an object literal overwrites an earlier one with the same name. In our example, Box does this with style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }} (caller’s style spread after the default, so it can override fontStyle) and className={box ${className}} for classes.
function Box({ className = &#039;&#039;, style, ...rest }: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt;) {
  return &lt;div className={`box ${className}`} style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }} {...rest} /&gt;
}
Q: What’s the benefit of a typed size prop over exposing raw class names?
A: More generally, this is an API-design principle: hide implementation details (like specific CSS class names) behind a small, typed surface so callers can’t couple to internals that might change, and so they get editor autocomplete instead of having to memorize magic strings. In our example, Box accepts size?: &#039;small&#039; | &#039;medium&#039; | &#039;large&#039; and internally maps it to box--${size}, keeping that class-naming scheme private to the component.
Q: What problem does a library like clsx solve?
A: In general, conditionally combining CSS class names by hand ([cond &amp;&amp; &#039;class&#039;, ...].filter(Boolean).join(&#039; &#039;)) is a common but easy-to-get-wrong pattern once you have more than a couple of conditions — clsx (or its predecessor classnames) standardizes it, including an object syntax for toggling classes by boolean flags. In our example, Box’s [box, sizeClassName, className].filter(Boolean).join(&#039; &#039;) is exactly the pattern clsx(&#039;box&#039;, size &amp;&amp; box—${size}, className) would replace.
Styling

Lesson

&lt;div className=&quot;box box--small&quot; style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, backgroundColor: &#039;lightblue&#039; }}&gt;
  small lightblue box
&lt;/div&gt;

💬 In HTML, inline style is a single string of CSS; in JSX it’s an object, with camelCased property names (matching the DOM’s CSSStyleDeclaration, e.g. background-color → backgroundColor). The style={{ ... }} double-curly isn’t special syntax — it’s just a JSX expression ({}) containing an object literal ({}). Numeric values (e.g. marginTop: 20) get px appended automatically.

Custom Component

Lesson

function Box({ className = &#039;&#039;, style, ...rest }: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt;) {
  return (
    &lt;div
      className={`box ${className}`}
      style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }}
      {...rest}
    /&gt;
  )
}

💬 React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt; borrows the full prop type (all valid HTML attributes) for a given intrinsic element — the standard way to type a component that wraps and forwards props to a native element. Plucking off className and style lets Box merge its own defaults with whatever the caller passes, while ...rest (including children) is spread onto the underlying div untouched. Placement controls precedence: spreading style after fontStyle lets the caller override it; putting fontStyle after the spread would make it non-overridable.

Size Prop

Lesson

function Box({
  className = &#039;&#039;,
  style,
  size,
  ...rest
}: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt; &amp; { size?: &#039;small&#039; | &#039;medium&#039; | &#039;large&#039; }) {
  const sizeClassName = size ? `box--${size}` : &#039;&#039;
  return (
    &lt;div
      className={[`box`, sizeClassName, className].filter(Boolean).join(&#039; &#039;)}
      style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }}
      {...rest}
    /&gt;
  )
}

// &lt;Box size=&quot;small&quot; style={{ backgroundColor: &#039;lightblue&#039; }}&gt;small lightblue box&lt;/Box&gt;

💬 Rather than making callers know which raw class name produces which visual effect, the component exposes a typed size prop and translates it internally — the styling implementation (box--small etc.) becomes a private detail of Box, and callers get autocomplete instead of having to memorize class name strings. React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt; &amp; { size?: ... } is a TypeScript intersection — combining “all div props” with one custom prop.


💬 The [...].filter(Boolean).join(&#039; &#039;) pattern above is exactly what a tiny, ubiquitous utility library — clsx (or its predecessor classnames) — exists to replace: clsx(&#039;box&#039;, size &amp;&amp; box—${size}, className). It also handles object syntax for toggling classes by condition (clsx({ &#039;is-active&#039;: isActive })). Worth reaching for as soon as conditional class logic grows past two or three classes, rather than reinventing this join pattern in every component.

Using JSX

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

JSX is HTML-like syntactic sugar that compiles to createElement calls. Because browsers can’t evaluate JSX natively, it needs a compiler (Babel, or a build tool in real apps) to convert it first.
const element = &lt;h1 id=&quot;greeting&quot;&gt;Hey there&lt;/h1&gt;

// ↓ compiles to ↓

const element = createElement(&#039;h1&#039;, { id: &#039;greeting&#039;, children: &#039;Hey there&#039; })

💬 The single most useful skill for getting fluent with JSX: train your brain to see the compiled createElement version whenever you look at JSX, and vice versa.

The Modern (Automatic) JSX Runtime

💬 This workshop uses Babel’s classic transform, which is why every example compiles to React.createElement(...) and needs React in scope. Modern tooling (Vite, Next.js, and current Babel/TypeScript defaults) uses the newer automatic runtime instead: JSX compiles to calls like _jsx(&#039;h1&#039;, { id: &#039;greeting&#039;, children: &#039;Hey there&#039; }), importing jsx/jsxs automatically from react/jsx-runtime at the top of the file — code you never write yourself. That’s the real reason modern React files don’t need import React from &#039;react&#039; just to use JSX anymore; it’s not that JSX stopped needing a runtime, it’s that the import is now injected by the compiler instead of written by hand.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What is JSX, and why do browsers need help running it?
A: In general, JSX is HTML-like syntactic sugar for nested function calls that describe UI — it isn’t valid JavaScript on its own, so a compiler (Babel, SWC, TypeScript, or whatever a bundler uses) must transform it into plain JS calls before a browser (or Node) can run it. In our example, &lt;h1 id=&quot;greeting&quot;&gt;Hey there&lt;/h1&gt; compiles to createElement(&#039;h1&#039;, { id: &#039;greeting&#039;, children: &#039;Hey there&#039; }).
const element = &lt;h1 id=&quot;greeting&quot;&gt;Hey there&lt;/h1&gt;
// compiles to: createElement(&#039;h1&#039;, { id: &#039;greeting&#039;, children: &#039;Hey there&#039; })
Q: Why don’t modern React files need import React from &#039;react&#039; just to use JSX?
A: Generally, this changed because of a compiler-level shift: the older classic JSX transform compiled tags to React.createElement(...), requiring React to be in scope, whereas the newer automatic runtime (default in modern Vite/Next.js/Babel/TS configs) compiles to calls like _jsx(...) and auto-injects the needed jsx/jsxs import from react/jsx-runtime — code you never write by hand. In our example/this workshop specifically, the classic transform is used on purpose (to keep createElement visible), which is why import * as React is still required there.
Q: What can go inside JSX curly braces {}?
A: As a general JS rule carried into JSX, {} is a doorway into JavaScript, but only for expressions — things that evaluate to a value (a variable, a ternary, an IIFE) — never statements (if, for, let x = 1), since JSX curlies compile to a single expression’s worth of function arguments. This applies identically in children position and in prop/attribute position. In our example, {className} and {children} interpolate variables, and a conditional render would use a ternary or &amp;&amp;, not a bare if.
const className = &#039;container&#039;
const element = &lt;div className={className}&gt;{children}&lt;/div&gt;
Q: How does prop spreading resolve conflicts in JSX?
A: Generally, JSX spread behaves exactly like object spread (Object.assign semantics) — whichever prop appears later in source order wins a conflict, whether that’s a spread object or an explicit prop. In our example, &lt;div {...props} className=&quot;override-wins&quot; /&gt; lets the explicit className win over whatever was in props; reversing the order (&lt;div className=&quot;x&quot; {...props} /&gt;) would let the spread win instead — including for children.
Q: Why does {count &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;} sometimes render a stray 0?
A: This is a general JS/React gotcha, not specific to any one component: React treats false, null, and undefined as “render nothing,” but 0 and NaN are still valid, visible children in JS’s &amp;&amp; short-circuit — so when the left-hand value is falsy-but-numeric (0), it gets printed literally instead of hiding the badge. In our example, {count &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;} prints a stray 0 when count is 0; the fix is forcing a real boolean: {count &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;}.
Q: Why do Fragments (&lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt;) exist?
A: More generally, a single JavaScript expression can only evaluate to one value, and createElement(...)/JSX ultimately has to return one thing — so returning multiple sibling elements from a component needs some wrapping container, and Fragments provide one that adds zero extra DOM nodes. In our example, wrapping a &lt;p&gt; and a &lt;ul&gt; in &lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt; groups them as one React element without adding a wrapper div that could break CSS Grid/Flexbox/table layouts relying on direct child structure.
const element = (
  &lt;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Here&#039;s Sam&#039;s favorite food:&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;ul className=&quot;sams-food&quot;&gt;...&lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/&gt;
)
Compiling JSX

Lesson

To run JSX in the browser without a build step, load Babel Standalone and mark the script for it to compile:
&lt;script src=&quot;/babel-standalone.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script type=&quot;text/babel&quot; data-type=&quot;module&quot;&gt;
  import * as React from &#039;/react.js&#039;
  const element = &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;Hello World&lt;/div&gt;
  // ...
&lt;/script&gt;

💬 type=&quot;text/babel&quot; tells Babel to transpile the script (the browser ignores it otherwise, since it isn’t a real type it understands); data-type=&quot;module&quot; tells Babel to re-emit the compiled output as an ES module so import keeps working. import * as React (rather than import { createElement }) matters because the compiler rewrites JSX to React.createElement(...), so React needs to be in scope.

Interpolation

Lesson

const className = &#039;container&#039;
const children = &#039;Hello World&#039;
const element = &lt;div className={className}&gt;{children}&lt;/div&gt;

💬 Curly braces {} are a doorway from “JSX land” into “JavaScript land” — anything inside must be an expression (something that evaluates to a value), never a statement. You can’t put an if or a for loop directly inside {}, but a ternary or an immediately-invoked arrow function works, because those are expressions. This works the same in the children position and in attribute/prop position.

Spread props

Lesson

const props = { children: &#039;Hello World&#039;, className: &#039;container&#039; }
const element = &lt;div {...props} className=&quot;override-wins&quot; /&gt;

💬 JSX prop spreading behaves exactly like object spread (Object.assign under the hood) — whatever comes later wins in a conflict. So &lt;div {...props} className=&quot;x&quot; /&gt; lets className=&quot;x&quot; override props.className, while &lt;div className=&quot;x&quot; {...props} /&gt; lets props.className win instead. The same rule applies to children: text between the opening/closing tags overrides a children prop set earlier in the spread.

Nesting JSX

Lesson

const element = (
  &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Here&#039;s Sam&#039;s favorite food:&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;ul className=&quot;sams-food&quot;&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;Green eggs&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;li&gt;Ham&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
)

💬 This is the direct payoff of JSX over raw createElement — you can paste in near-verbatim HTML. The one gotcha: JSX props map to DOM properties, not HTML attributes (class → className, for → htmlFor, tabindex → tabIndex). React warns in the console whenever you use the attribute name instead of the property name.


💬 A related gotcha for conditional rendering: React renders false, null, and undefined as nothing at all, but 0 and NaN still render (they’re valid, visible children). So {count &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;} silently prints a stray 0 on the page when count is 0 — a very common real-world bug. The idiomatic fix is to make the condition explicitly boolean: {count &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;}.

Fragments

Lesson

const element = (
  &lt;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Here&#039;s Sam&#039;s favorite food:&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;ul className=&quot;sams-food&quot;&gt;...&lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/&gt;
)

💬 &lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt; (shorthand for &lt;React.Fragment&gt;...&lt;/React.Fragment&gt;) groups sibling elements without adding a wrapper DOM node. It exists because a single JavaScript expression can’t evaluate to two separate values — createElement(...), createElement(...) isn’t valid, so you need a container element without an actual DOM representation. Useful anywhere the DOM structure matters for CSS (Grid, Flexbox, tables) and an extra wrapper div would break the layout.

Lifting State

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Hooks
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 3, “Lifting State”) plus his blog post
State Colocation will make your React app faster
and react.dev’s Sharing State Between Components.
Companion to Managing UI State (the same search/filter app this
exercise restructures). Notes and clarifications by me are marked with
&gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What does “lifting state” mean, and when do you need it?
A: Moving a useState call up to the lowest common parent of every
component that needs to read or write it — the fix for “two sibling
components need to share state,” since siblings can’t hand data to each
other directly, only through something that renders both of them:
// Counter owns its own state — CountDisplay, a sibling, can&#039;t see it
function App() {
  return &lt;div&gt;&lt;Counter /&gt;&lt;CountDisplay /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
}

// Fix: App owns the state, passes it (and the setter) down to both
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Counter count={count} setCount={setCount} /&gt;
      &lt;CountDisplay count={count} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Q: What did step lift actually break that needed fixing?
A: The workshop app was refactored into separate Form/MatchingPosts
components, but the query state and its useEffect (which listens for
popstate — browser back/forward — and re-syncs query from the URL) got
left in the wrong place relative to who needs them. The fix is to keep
query, setQuery, and the popstate effect in App (the common parent
of Form and MatchingPosts) and pass query/setQuery down as props:
function App() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState(getQueryParam)
  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const updateQuery = () =&gt; setQuery(getQueryParam())
    window.addEventListener(&#039;popstate&#039;, updateQuery)
    return () =&gt; window.removeEventListener(&#039;popstate&#039;, updateQuery)
  }, [])
  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;app&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Form query={query} setQuery={setQuery} /&gt;
      &lt;MatchingPosts query={query} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Note that the useEffect moved too, not just the state — an effect that
reads/writes a piece of state generally has to live wherever that state
lives, for the same reason the state itself had to move.
Q: Step lift-array adds a “favorite” heart button per post, and a
sort-by-favorite feature. Why does favorites need to live in
MatchingPosts instead of each Card?
A: Because sorting the whole list by favorite status requires comparing
every card’s favorited state against every other card’s — a computation
that needs to see all of them at once, which is only possible from their
common parent. Each Card can’t sort a list it doesn’t have access to:
function MatchingPosts({ query }: { query: string }) {
  const matchingPosts = getMatchingPosts(query)
  const [favorites, setFavorites] = useState&lt;Array&lt;string&gt;&gt;([])
  return (
    &lt;ul className=&quot;post-list&quot;&gt;
      {matchingPosts
        .sort((a, b) =&gt; {
          const aFav = favorites.includes(a.id)
          const bFav = favorites.includes(b.id)
          return aFav === bFav ? 0 : aFav ? -1 : 1
        })
        .map((post) =&gt; (
          &lt;Card key={post.id} post={post} isFavorited={favorites.includes(post.id)}
            onFavoriteClick={(fav) =&gt; {
              setFavorites(fav ? [...favorites, post.id] : favorites.filter((id) =&gt; id !== post.id))
            }} /&gt;
        ))}
    &lt;/ul&gt;
  )
}
Card becomes a plain, stateless renderer of isFavorited +
onFavoriteClick — it no longer owns anything, exactly the composition
tradeoff [[Composition and Layout Components]] covers from a different
angle (a component gets simpler by giving up ownership of data it doesn’t
itself need to own).
Q: Step colocate removes the sort-by-favorite feature. Why move
favorites back down to Card instead of just leaving it in
MatchingPosts?
A: Because the only reason it had to live in MatchingPosts was the sort
— once sorting is gone, nothing outside an individual Card reads or needs
another card’s favorited state. Leaving it lifted after the reason for
lifting it disappears is what colocation fixes:
function MatchingPosts({ query }: { query: string }) {
  const matchingPosts = getMatchingPosts(query)
  return (
    &lt;ul className=&quot;post-list&quot;&gt;
      {matchingPosts.map((post) =&gt; &lt;Card key={post.id} post={post} /&gt;)}
    &lt;/ul&gt;
  )
}

function Card({ post }: { post: BlogPost }) {
  const [isFavorited, setIsFavorited] = useState(false)
  // ...
}
Q: What’s the actual payoff, beyond “the code looks simpler”?
A: Two concrete things, both called out directly in the workshop’s own
solution notes: MatchingPosts and Card both get simpler (no more
prop-drilled isFavorited/onFavoriteClick, no array-membership check), and
— the performance angle — clicking one card’s favorite button now only
re-renders that card, not the whole MatchingPosts list, because the
state update happens inside Card itself rather than in its parent. The
trade-off, also noted in the workshop: colocating into Card means a
card’s favorited status is lost if it’s ever unmounted (e.g. filtered out by
a search), whereas keeping it in App (not MatchingPosts, which itself
unmounts/remounts cards on every search) would have survived that — real
persistence would need the database, which is out of scope here. Colocation
isn’t “always push state as low as possible, full stop” — it’s “push it
down to the lowest point that still keeps it alive for as long as it needs
to be.”
How lifting and colocating actually work, in the same tiny example
The workshop’s own README explains this with a minimal Counter/
CountDisplay example before ever touching the search-app code above, and
it’s worth walking through in full because it isolates the mechanism from
everything else going on in the bigger app.
Lifting: two siblings can’t talk directly
Start with Counter owning its own state, and a sibling CountDisplay that
needs to show that same count:
function App() {
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Counter /&gt;
      &lt;CountDisplay /&gt; {/* wants to show Counter&#039;s count, but can&#039;t see it */}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
  return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(count + 1)}&gt;Increment&lt;/button&gt;
}

function CountDisplay() {
  return &lt;div&gt;Count: 0&lt;/div&gt; {/* stuck at 0 forever */}
}
CountDisplay has no path to Counter’s state — React data flow is
top-down through props, and siblings sit next to each other in the tree with
no direct edge between them. The only component with a wire to both of
them is their parent, App. Lifting is just moving the useState call up
to that parent and threading it back down to both:
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;Counter count={count} setCount={setCount} /&gt;
      &lt;CountDisplay count={count} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

function Counter({ count, setCount }) {
  return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(count + 1)}&gt;Increment&lt;/button&gt;
}

function CountDisplay({ count }) {
  return &lt;div&gt;Count: {count}&lt;/div&gt;
}
Nothing about useState itself changed — it’s the same hook, called in a
different component. What changed is which component’s re-render a call
to setCount now triggers: previously only Counter re-rendered on a
click; now App re-renders (since App owns the state), which means
Counter and CountDisplay both re-render as App’s children. That’s
the real mechanism, and it’s also exactly why colocating later is worth
doing — every level state gets lifted through widens the set of components
that have to re-render when it changes.
Colocating: undoing the lift once only one consumer is left
Now suppose CountDisplay goes away entirely, and the count only ever
needs to show up inside Counter’s own button. The lifted version still
works — App can keep owning count and pass it to the one remaining
consumer — but nothing outside Counter reads it anymore:
// Still works, but App is now managing state only Counter cares about
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
  return &lt;Counter count={count} setCount={setCount} /&gt;
}
function Counter({ count, setCount }) {
  return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(count + 1)}&gt;Increment ({count})&lt;/button&gt;
}
Colocating moves it back down to the one component that actually needs it:
function App() {
  return &lt;Counter /&gt;
}
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
  return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(count + 1)}&gt;Increment ({count})&lt;/button&gt;
}
As the workshop’s README puts it: “Novice React developers learn to lift
state pretty early, but experienced React developers know how to recognize
when state can be ‘pushed down.’” Lifting is the beginner-friendly fix for
“siblings need to share state”; colocating is the second, less obvious
skill — recognizing when the reason a value was lifted no longer applies.
Why colocation is a performance technique, not just tidiness
Kent C. Dodds’s post State Colocation will make your React app faster
makes the performance case directly with a DogName example: an input’s
own typed value, if kept in a parent alongside an unrelated slow-rendering
sibling component, forces that slow sibling to be checked for updates on
every keystroke — not because the slow component uses the input’s value,
but simply because it’s a descendant of the component whose state just
changed. Moving the input’s state down into the component that actually
owns the input removes the slow sibling from that re-render’s scope
entirely, since it’s no longer a descendant of anything that changed. As
Kent puts it, “the best way to make something fast is to do less stuff” —
and narrowing which subtree has to be checked on a state change is a
direct way to do less stuff.
His decision framework, condensed: reach for useState where a value is
first needed; if only one component ever reads or writes it, that’s where
it stays; if more than one component needs it, lift it to their lowest
common parent; only reach for Context once prop-drilling that lifted value
through several uninterested layers becomes the actual pain point (compare
Composition and Layout Components, which is Kent’s own preferred
alternative to reaching for Context at that stage).
The favorites example, traced against this framework
The workshop’s lift-array/colocate steps apply exactly this framework to
a real feature, not a toy counter — which is why the Q&amp;A section above’s
favorites walkthrough is worth reading against this same “who actually
needs to read this” question: it lives in MatchingPosts for exactly as
long as the sort-by-favorite feature needs to compare every card against
every other card, and moves back into Card the moment that comparison is
no longer part of the app — the same lift → colocate cycle as Counter/
CountDisplay above, just with a real feature attached to explain why
each move happens when it does.
Rules and caveats

Lift only as far as the nearest common parent that actually needs it —
not further. Lifting favorites all the way to App when only
MatchingPosts and Card ever touch it would work, but it’s an
unnecessary hop; MatchingPosts is already the lowest common ancestor.
Colocate once a lifted value no longer has multiple consumers. The
signal isn’t “can I move this down” (you almost always can, code-wise) —
it’s “does anything outside this component still need to read or write
it.” Once the answer is no, colocating simplifies the parent, simplifies
the child’s own prop signature, and narrows the re-render scope to just
that child.
An effect that depends on a piece of state has to move with it. The
popstate listener in this exercise isn’t independent of query — it
exists specifically to update query — so it lives wherever query
lives, the same rule that governs where the state itself belongs.
Colocating trades away lifetime, not just position. State inside a
child is lost whenever that child unmounts — sorting, filtering, or
conditionally rendering the child will wipe it. Weigh that against the
simplicity/performance win before colocating something that genuinely
needs to survive its owner being removed and re-added.

Sources

EpicWeb React Hooks workshop, exercise 03.lifting-state (steps lift,
lift-array, colocate)
Kent C. Dodds — State Colocation will make your React app faster
react.dev — Sharing State Between Components

useState vs useReducer

Article by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.


Interview Q&amp;A
Q: When should you use useState vs useReducer?
A: useState and useReducer are both just state-management primitives — useState is actually implemented as a thin wrapper over useReducer internally, so it’s a spectrum, not two unrelated tools. The general rule: reach for useState for state that’s simple and independent (a boolean, a string, an isolated value), and reach for useReducer once you have several related values that change together, a fixed set of named transitions (a state machine, e.g. idle → loading → success | error), or update logic complex/branchy enough that you want it out of the component body and independently testable. In our example, useDarkMode is one independent value → useState wins; useUndo has past/present/future that must always move together → useReducer wins.
// independent value → useState
const [mode, setMode] = useState(&#039;light&#039;)

// several values that change together, named transitions → useReducer
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(undoReducer, { past: [], present, future: [] })
dispatch({ type: &#039;UNDO&#039; })
Q: Is useReducer strictly more powerful than useState?
A: Yes, structurally — useState(init) behaves the same as useReducer with a reducer that just returns whatever new value it’s given, which is exactly how React implements useState under the hood. That means anything expressible with useState can be rewritten with useReducer, but not everything reducer logic can naturally express (multiple coordinated fields, action-based transitions) is comfortable to hand-roll with several separate useState calls. In our example, Version C of useDarkMode deliberately reimplements useState’s exact behavior with useReducer to prove the point — and it’s strictly worse for that use case, since simplifying a reducer this far just reinvents useState with more ceremony.
// useState(init) is roughly:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer((_, newValue) =&gt; newValue, init)
const setState = (v) =&gt; dispatch(typeof v === &#039;function&#039; ? v(state) : v)
Q: What bug can appear when related state is split across multiple useState calls, and how does useReducer avoid it?
A: This is the classic stale closure problem: any callback (an event handler, an effect, a memoized function) captures the values of state variables from the render it was created in, so if two updates to related state fire within the same render cycle — before a re-render lets the closures refresh — the second one can read and act on outdated values from the first, silently clobbering it. useReducer sidesteps this by design: the reducer always receives the current, just-committed state as an argument from React itself, not from a closure, so there’s nothing to go stale. (A callback-form setState(prev =&gt; next) fixes the same bug for useState, but you have to remember to do it every time; a reducer makes it the only option.) In our example, useUndo’s buggy useState version loses an entry when two useEffects each call set() in the same render pass — both read present: &#039;first&#039; from their own stale closures — while the useReducer version is immune because dispatch never reads state itself.
// stale-closure-safe update, either approach:
setState((prevState) =&gt; ({ ...prevState, present: newPresent })) // useState, callback form
dispatch({ type: &#039;SET&#039;, newPresent }) // useReducer — always safe by construction
Q: Why is dispatch from useReducer useful for passing updates through context?
A: Both useState’s setter and useReducer’s dispatch are guaranteed by React to keep the same function identity across renders — but dispatch is often preferred for context because it’s a single, generic entry point (“send an action”) rather than many distinct setters, which makes it easy to memoize and pass down as one stable value. Since it never changes reference, a component that only calls dispatch (and doesn’t read the state itself) can be wrapped in React.memo and will skip re-rendering when the state updates — only components that actually subscribe to the state value re-render. In our example, the note’s “Quick decision guide” calls this out explicitly as a useReducer-favoring case: “Passing updater down through context → useReducer (stable dispatch).”
const DispatchContext = createContext(null)
// A component that only calls dispatch(...) via this context never
// re-renders when state changes, since dispatch&#039;s identity never changes.
Q: Are reducers easier to test than useState-based logic?
A: Generally yes — a reducer is a pure function (state, action) =&gt; newState with no hooks, no React runtime, and no DOM involved, so every state transition can be exercised with a plain function call and a plain assertion, independent of rendering anything. Logic spread across several useCallbacks closing over useState values is coupled to React’s render cycle, so it typically has to be tested by rendering the component/hook (e.g. with @testing-library/react-hooks) and simulating interactions. In our example, undoReducer can be tested directly as undoReducer(state, { type: &#039;UNDO&#039; }) with no component involved at all, whereas the equivalent useUndo useState version needs a full render to verify.
expect(undoReducer(state, { type: &#039;UNDO&#039; })).toEqual(expectedState)

For a deep dive on why the useUndo useState bug happens and the broader re-rendering model it illustrates, see React Re-rendering.

Framing the Question
Whenever two things seem to do the same thing, people ask: “When do I use one over the other?” There are usually two reasons multiple approaches exist:

One is outdated (e.g. class components vs function components — class is the old way).
Each involves trade-offs suited to different scenarios.

useState and useReducer are case 2. Neither is better — they are tuned for different situations.

The Two Rules
Kent’s answer boils down to two sentences:

“When it’s just an independent element of state you’re managing: useState”
“When one element of your state relies on the value of another element of your state in order to update: useReducer”


💬 “Relies on the value of another element” means: to compute the next value of state A, you need to read the current value of state B. When that’s the case, having them in separate useState calls creates a closure-bug risk — see the useUndo section below.


Example 1 — useDarkMode
This example shows a case where useState is the right choice.
Version A: with useState ✅
function useDarkMode() {
  const preferDarkQuery = &#039;(prefers-color-scheme: dark)&#039;
  const [mode, setMode] = React.useState(
    () =&gt;
      window.localStorage.getItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;) ||
      (window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery).matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;),
  )

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const mediaQuery = window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery)
    const handleChange = () =&gt; setMode(mediaQuery.matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;)
    mediaQuery.addListener(handleChange)
    return () =&gt; mediaQuery.removeListener(handleChange)
  }, [])

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    window.localStorage.setItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;, mode)
  }, [mode])

  return [mode, setMode]
}

💬 The lazy initializer (the () =&gt; function passed to useState) runs only on mount — it reads localStorage and the system media query once to set the initial value. The first effect syncs back to localStorage whenever mode changes. The second effect listens to OS-level dark-mode changes (e.g. the user switches their system theme). Clean and flat.

Version B: with useReducer — verbose Redux-style ❌
const preferDarkQuery = &#039;(prefers-color-scheme: dark)&#039;

function darkModeReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case &#039;MEDIA_CHANGE&#039;: {
      return { ...state, mode: action.mode }
    }
    case &#039;SET_MODE&#039;: {
      return { ...state, mode: action.mode }
    }
    default: {
      throw new Error(`Unhandled action type: ${action.type}`)
    }
  }
}

function init() {
  return {
    mode:
      window.localStorage.getItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;) ||
      (window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery).matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;),
  }
}

function useDarkMode() {
  const [state, dispatch] = React.useReducer(
    darkModeReducer,
    { mode: &#039;light&#039; },
    init,
  )
  const { mode } = state

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const mediaQuery = window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery)
    const handleChange = () =&gt;
      dispatch({
        type: &#039;MEDIA_CHANGE&#039;,
        mode: mediaQuery.matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;,
      })
    mediaQuery.addListener(handleChange)
    return () =&gt; mediaQuery.removeListener(handleChange)
  }, [])

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    window.localStorage.setItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;, mode)
  }, [mode])

  const setMode = React.useCallback(
    (newMode) =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;SET_MODE&#039;, mode: newMode }),
    [],
  )

  return [mode, setMode]
}

💬 useReducer takes three arguments: (reducer, initialArg, init). When you pass init, React calls init(initialArg) to compute the real initial state — equivalent to useState’s lazy initializer. This pattern is useful when you want to reset state back to its initial value later (just dispatch and call init again inside the reducer). Here it’s just added ceremony.

Version C: with useReducer — simplified, but still worse ❌
function useDarkMode() {
  const preferDarkQuery = &#039;(prefers-color-scheme: dark)&#039;
  const [mode, setMode] = React.useReducer(
    (prevMode, nextMode) =&gt;
      typeof nextMode === &#039;function&#039; ? nextMode(prevMode) : nextMode,
    &#039;light&#039;,
    () =&gt;
      window.localStorage.getItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;) ||
      (window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery).matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;),
  )

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const mediaQuery = window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery)
    const handleChange = () =&gt; setMode(mediaQuery.matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;)
    mediaQuery.addListener(handleChange)
    return () =&gt; mediaQuery.removeListener(handleChange)
  }, [])

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    window.localStorage.setItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;, mode)
  }, [mode])

  return [mode, setMode]
}

💬 This reducer mimics useState exactly: if you dispatch a function, call it with previous state; otherwise replace state with the new value. This is literally how useState works internally. Kent’s point: if you’ve simplified useReducer this much, you’ve just reinvented useState — and the original useState version is still clearer.

Verdict: mode is a single independent value. No other state depends on it, and it doesn’t depend on any other state. useState wins.

Example 2 — useUndo
This example shows a case where useReducer is the right choice.
useUndo tracks a value through time with three arrays: past (history), present (current), and future (for redo).
Version A: useState — the buggy version ❌
function useUndo(initialPresent) {
  const [past, setPast] = React.useState([])
  const [present, setPresent] = React.useState(initialPresent)
  const [future, setFuture] = React.useState([])

  const canUndo = past.length !== 0
  const canRedo = future.length !== 0

  const undo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    if (!canUndo) return

    const previous = past[past.length - 1]
    const newPast = past.slice(0, past.length - 1)

    setPast(newPast)
    setPresent(previous)
    setFuture([present, ...future])
  }, [canUndo, future, past, present])

  const redo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    if (!canRedo) return

    const next = future[0]
    const newFuture = future.slice(1)

    setPast([...past, present])
    setPresent(next)
    setFuture(newFuture)
  }, [canRedo, future, past, present])

  const set = React.useCallback(
    (newPresent) =&gt; {
      if (newPresent === present) {
        return
      }
      setPast([...past, present])
      setPresent(newPresent)
      setFuture([])
    },
    [past, present],
  )

  const reset = React.useCallback((newPresent) =&gt; {
    setPast([])
    setPresent(newPresent)
    setFuture([])
  }, [])

  return [
    { past, present, future },
    { set, reset, undo, redo, canUndo, canRedo },
  ]
}
The bug surfaces here:
function Example() {
  const [state, { set }] = useUndo(&#039;first&#039;)

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    set(&#039;second&#039;)
  }, [])

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    set(&#039;third&#039;)
  }, [])

  return &lt;pre&gt;{JSON.stringify(state, null, 2)}&lt;/pre&gt;
}

💬 Why this breaks: Each useCallback captures past, present, and future from the render in which it was created. When set(&#039;second&#039;) runs, it reads past = [] and present = &#039;first&#039; — correct. But set(&#039;third&#039;) runs in the same render cycle, so it still reads the same stale snapshot: past = [] and present = &#039;first&#039;. The second call overwrites, losing the &#039;second&#039; entry entirely.
If you add set to the dependency arrays of both effects to silence the lint warning, the effects now re-run whenever set changes. But set depends on past and present, which change when set runs — infinite loop. The two are chasing each other.

Version B: useState — the fixed version ⚠️
function useUndo(initialPresent) {
  const [state, setState] = React.useState({
    past: [],
    present: initialPresent,
    future: [],
  })

  const canUndo = state.past.length !== 0
  const canRedo = state.future.length !== 0

  const undo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    setState((currentState) =&gt; {
      const { past, present, future } = currentState

      if (past.length === 0) return currentState

      const previous = past[past.length - 1]
      const newPast = past.slice(0, past.length - 1)

      return {
        past: newPast,
        present: previous,
        future: [present, ...future],
      }
    })
  }, [])

  const redo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    setState((currentState) =&gt; {
      const { past, present, future } = currentState

      if (future.length === 0) return currentState

      const next = future[0]
      const newFuture = future.slice(1)

      return {
        past: [...past, present],
        present: next,
        future: newFuture,
      }
    })
  }, [])

  const set = React.useCallback((newPresent) =&gt; {
    setState((currentState) =&gt; {
      const { present, past } = currentState
      if (newPresent === present) return currentState

      return {
        past: [...past, present],
        present: newPresent,
        future: [],
      }
    })
  }, [])

  const reset = React.useCallback((newPresent) =&gt; {
    setState(() =&gt; ({
      past: [],
      present: newPresent,
      future: [],
    }))
  }, [])

  return [state, { set, reset, undo, redo, canUndo, canRedo }]
}

💬 How the fix works: Instead of reading past/present/future from the closure, we use the state updater callback form of setState(currentState =&gt; nextState). React guarantees that currentState is the most recent committed state — not a stale snapshot. So now both effects see the correct, up-to-date values. This also means set, undo, and redo have empty dependency arrays [] and never change reference, which also solves the infinite loop.
This works! But it required: (1) collapsing three separate useState calls into one object, (2) rewriting every updater as a callback, and (3) performing all the calculations inside the callback instead of in the closure. It’s correct but noticeably awkward.

Version C: useReducer — natural fit ✅
const UNDO = &#039;UNDO&#039;
const REDO = &#039;REDO&#039;
const SET = &#039;SET&#039;
const RESET = &#039;RESET&#039;

function undoReducer(state, action) {
  const { past, present, future } = state
  const { type, newPresent } = action

  switch (type) {
    case UNDO: {
      if (past.length === 0) return state

      const previous = past[past.length - 1]
      const newPast = past.slice(0, past.length - 1)

      return {
        past: newPast,
        present: previous,
        future: [present, ...future],
      }
    }

    case REDO: {
      if (future.length === 0) return state

      const next = future[0]
      const newFuture = future.slice(1)

      return {
        past: [...past, present],
        present: next,
        future: newFuture,
      }
    }

    case SET: {
      if (newPresent === present) return state

      return {
        past: [...past, present],
        present: newPresent,
        future: [],
      }
    }

    case RESET: {
      return {
        past: [],
        present: newPresent,
        future: [],
      }
    }
  }
}

function useUndo(initialPresent) {
  const [state, dispatch] = React.useReducer(undoReducer, {
    past: [],
    present: initialPresent,
    future: [],
  })

  const canUndo = state.past.length !== 0
  const canRedo = state.future.length !== 0
  const undo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; dispatch({ type: UNDO }), [])
  const redo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; dispatch({ type: REDO }), [])
  const set = React.useCallback(
    (newPresent) =&gt; dispatch({ type: SET, newPresent }),
    [],
  )
  const reset = React.useCallback(
    (newPresent) =&gt; dispatch({ type: RESET, newPresent }),
    [],
  )

  return [state, { set, reset, undo, redo, canUndo, canRedo }]
}

💬 Notice how clean the hook body becomes. Each callback just dispatches — it never reads state at all. The reducer gets the current state as its first argument from React directly, so there’s never a stale closure. The stale-closure problem doesn’t arise because we never need to capture state in the closure in the first place.
Kent’s key observation: “We wouldn’t have even considered adding anything to our dependency array because those functions are so simple they don’t need anything. All the logic lives in our reducer. That helps us avoid the issue naturally.”

Verdict: past, present, and future always change together as a unit. They belong in one reducer.

Conclusion (Kent’s Words)

“Honestly, even the ‘rules’ are subjective because as I demonstrated, you can do everything you want with either one.”


“You can absolutely use useState in the same component or hook that’s using useReducer.”


“Separate state logically by domain. If it changes together, it’s likely better to keep together in a reducer. If something is pretty independent from other elements of state, then putting it with other elements of state is just adding unnecessary complexity.”


“In general, I suggest starting with useState, and moving to useReducer when you notice elements of state need to change together.”


My Additional Angles
Reducers are pure functions → easy to test
A reducer is just (state, action) =&gt; newState — no hooks, no DOM, no React. You can test every state transition with a plain function call:
expect(undoReducer(state, { type: &#039;UNDO&#039; })).toEqual(expectedState)
With useState logic spread across multiple useCallback closures, you can only test through a rendered component.
dispatch has a stable reference
useReducer returns [state, dispatch]. The dispatch function is stable — it never changes between renders (same guarantee as setState). This matters when passing updaters through context: components that only consume dispatch won’t re-render when state changes, because dispatch is the same reference every time.
useState is useReducer under the hood
React implements useState as a simplified useReducer internally. Conceptually:
// useState(init) is roughly:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer((_, newValue) =&gt; newValue, init)
const setState = (v) =&gt; dispatch(typeof v === &#039;function&#039; ? v(state) : v)
Everything useState can do, useReducer can do — but not vice versa. Kent’s Version C above is the proof.
Think in state machines
When your state has a fixed set of valid values and named transitions between them (idle → loading → success | error), useReducer maps to that model directly. Each case in the switch is one edge in the graph. This makes impossible states easier to prevent — you only return shapes you’ve explicitly coded.
Quick decision guide
Single value, no dependencies         → useState
Multiple values that move together    → useReducer
Next state depends on other state     → useReducer
Want to unit-test state logic alone   → useReducer
Passing updater down through context  → useReducer (stable dispatch)

Context

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Performance
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 1, “Element Optimization”, step 3
context) plus the react.dev memo
reference on context. Part of Element Optimization. Previous step:
Element Props. Next step: Memoize Elements. My own notes and
clarifications are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: If a component is memoized (or its element is reused), does it still re-render when a context it reads changes?
A: Yes, always. Memoization only affects updates that come through
props. React tracks context consumers separately and schedules every
component that read a changed context, whatever its props. react.dev:
“Memoized components will still re-render when a context they consume
changes.” In our example, the hoisted &lt;Footer /&gt; skips counter clicks but
updates when the color changes, because it reads ColorContext.
Q: How does React decide whether a context value changed?
A: It compares the provider’s previous and next value with Object.is.
Primitives compare by value. Objects, arrays, and functions compare by
reference. In our example value={color} is a string, so
&#039;black&#039; → &#039;black&#039; counts as unchanged.
Q: Why is &lt;Ctx value={{ a, b }}&gt; a common performance bug?
A: { a, b } is a new object literal on every render of the provider’s
component, so Object.is(prev, next) is always false, and every
consumer re-renders every time the provider’s component renders, even
when a and b didn’t change. Fix it by passing a primitive, splitting
into several contexts, or wrapping the value in useMemo.
Q: Should you move state into context just to avoid re-renders?
A: Only when it also makes sense for the data flow. Here it works because
it removes the element’s only dynamic prop, so the element can be hoisted
again. But context re-renders all its consumers on every change, and it
hides the data flow. The workshop’s closing reminder puts readability
first unless there’s a real performance problem.
The problem: unrelated parent state re-creates the element
App gained a second counter (appCount). Each click re-renders App,
which re-creates &lt;Footer color={color} /&gt;, so Footer re-renders even
though color didn’t change:
function Footer({ color }: { color: string }) {
	// 🐨 get the color from the ColorContext
	return &lt;footer style={{ color }}&gt;I am the ({color}) footer&lt;/footer&gt;
}

function App() {
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [appCount, setAppCount] = useState(0)
	// 🐨 wrap all this with the ColorContext provider and pass the color
	return (
		&lt;div&gt;
			{/* ...color buttons... */}
			&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setAppCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)}&gt;
				The app count is {appCount}
			&lt;/button&gt;
			{/* 🐨 remove the color prop and move this outside the component again */}
			&lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer color={color} /&gt;} /&gt;
		&lt;/div&gt;
	)
}
The workshop asks: “Have we lost the ability to take advantage of React’s
element optimization? No!”
The fix: route the dynamic value around the element
“Instead of accepting the color via props, we can place it in a context
provider that the Footer can consume. And with that, the footer no
longer accepts any dynamic props so we can move it outside the App
component again”:
const ColorContext = createContext&lt;string | null&gt;(null)

function useColor() {
	const color = use(ColorContext)
	if (!color) throw new Error(&#039;ColorContext not found&#039;)
	return color
}

function Footer() {
	const color = useColor()
	return &lt;footer style={{ color }}&gt;I am the ({color}) footer&lt;/footer&gt;
}

const footer = &lt;Footer /&gt;

function App() {
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [appCount, setAppCount] = useState(0)
	return (
		&lt;ColorContext value={color}&gt;
			&lt;div&gt;
				{/* ...color buttons... */}
				&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setAppCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)}&gt;
					The app count is {appCount}
				&lt;/button&gt;
				&lt;Main footer={footer} /&gt;
			&lt;/div&gt;
		&lt;/ColorContext&gt;
	)
}

💬 Two React 19 APIs appear here. &lt;ColorContext value&gt; renders the
context itself as the provider (no .Provider), and use(ColorContext)
reads context like useContext does.

Props and context are two separate update paths
Until this step, color travelled through the element’s props, and a
dynamic prop forces the element to be created during render. Context opens
a second channel that goes around the element: the value is read inside
Footer during its own render, never passed via props. That splits the
two jobs:




















Update pathWhat controls itIn this stepProps (parent re-render)the element’s props object identityhoisted → always the same → always skipsContextObject.is(prevValue, nextValue) on the providerchanges only when color changes → re-renders Footer
The workshop phrases the result as: “the only way it can be rerendered is
if the context changes (which is exactly what we want).”
Why it works: the objects across two renders
const footer = { type: Footer, props: {} /* P1 */ }   // module scope, once

// Click &quot;app count&quot;: App re-renders
//   provider value: Object.is(&#039;black&#039;, &#039;black&#039;) → true  → no context change
//   &lt;Main footer={footer} /&gt; has a new props literal { footer: #A } → Main() runs
//   Footer slot: P1 !== P1 → false, no context change → BAIL OUT

// Click &quot;Blue&quot;: App re-renders
//   provider value: Object.is(&#039;black&#039;, &#039;blue&#039;) → false → propagate:
//     React walks the subtree and marks every fiber that read ColorContext
//   Main() runs, returns footer again
//   Footer slot: P1 !== P1 → false... BUT the fiber is marked → Footer() runs
The props check passes both times. What differs is the context mark. This
matches the workshop test’s expectation that an app-count click calls only
App and Main.
Provider value identity
The provider uses the same equality rules as everything else in this
exercise. Here are three ways to write the provider, and what they cost:
// ✅ primitive: compares by value, consumers update only on real changes
&lt;ColorContext value={color}&gt;

// ❌ new object every App render: every consumer re-renders on EVERY App render,
//    including appCount clicks, which undoes this whole step
&lt;ThemeContext value={{ color, setColor }}&gt;

// ✅ stable object: same reference until color changes
const theme = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ color, setColor }), [color])
&lt;ThemeContext value={theme}&gt;
react.dev’s advice on memoized components that read context is along the
same lines: to update only when part of a context changes, read the
context in a parent and pass the specific piece down as a prop to a
memoized child. For the general causes of re-renders, see
React Re-rendering.
Context, step by step
// STEP 0: module load: footer = #A (props P1 = {}). Footer isn&#039;t called yet.

// STEP 1: mount. App(): color=&#039;black&#039;, appCount=0
//   returns &lt;ColorContext value=&quot;black&quot;&gt; … &lt;Main footer={#A}/&gt; …
// STEP 2: Main() returns #A. Footer() runs:
//   use(ColorContext) reads &#039;black&#039; AND records a dependency on ColorContext
// STEP 3: commit.

// STEP 4: click &quot;The app count is 0&quot; → setAppCount → update on App
// STEP 5: App() runs → new provider element, value still &#039;black&#039;
// STEP 6: provider: Object.is(&#039;black&#039;,&#039;black&#039;) → no propagation
// STEP 7: Main has new props { footer: #A } → Main() runs → returns #A
// STEP 8: Footer: P1 !== P1 false, no context mark → BAIL OUT

// STEP 9:  click &quot;Blue&quot; → setColor(&#039;blue&#039;) → App() runs
// STEP 10: provider: Object.is(&#039;black&#039;,&#039;blue&#039;) false → mark Footer&#039;s fiber
// STEP 11: Main() runs → returns #A
// STEP 12: Footer: props identical, but marked → Footer() runs,
//          useColor() returns &#039;blue&#039; → DOM color updates
Rules and caveats

All consumers re-render on any change. Keep context values narrow
(one color, not the whole app state), or split contexts by how often
they change.
Stabilize object values. Use a primitive, or useMemo the object.
An inline object literal re-renders every consumer on every provider
render.
Reading context makes a component depend on its provider. The
useColor hook throws a clear error when the provider is missing, which
is a good habit for any required context.
Don’t reach for context only for performance. Here it doubles as a
sensible data-flow choice (theme-like data). If it only obscures where
data comes from, prefer Memoize Components.

