Element Props

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Performance
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 1, “Element Optimization”, step 2
props) plus his post One simple trick to optimize React re-renders.
Part of Element Optimization. Previous step: Reusing Elements.
Next step: Context. It’s the same move as
Composition and Layout Components, seen as a performance tool. My own
notes and clarifications are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: How can passing a component as a prop (or as children) reduce re-renders?
A: An element’s identity changes only when the component that creates it
re-renders. If a parent creates the element and passes it to a child that
re-renders on its own (from its own state), the child keeps receiving the
same element object, so React skips it. In our example, App creates
&lt;Footer color={color} /&gt; and passes it to Main. Clicking Main’s own
counter re-renders only Main, so the footer isn’t re-created and isn’t
re-rendered.
Q: Does &lt;Parent&gt;&lt;Child /&gt;&lt;/Parent&gt; optimize anything compared to rendering &lt;Child /&gt; inside Parent?
A: Yes, when Parent has its own state. With children, &lt;Child /&gt; is
created by whoever renders &lt;Parent&gt;, so Parent’s state updates reuse
the same props.children object. When Parent renders &lt;Child /&gt;
itself, every Parent update creates a new element. children is just a
prop named children, so this is the same mechanism as the footer prop
here.
Q: Is this a performance technique or a design technique?
A: Both. It’s the composition pattern, which was introduced to avoid prop
drilling, and it gets the optimization “for free”. Kent’s advice in his post
is to reach for this restructuring before “coating codebases with
React.memo”. The workshop’s solution says: “This is the composition
pattern from the Advanced React Patterns workshop at work!”
Q: When does this stop helping?
A: When the component that creates the element re-renders just as often
as the one that receives it. In our example, as soon as App gets
unrelated state (next step, Context), every App render creates a new
footer element again.
The problem: the element is created by the component that re-renders most
The footer now takes a color prop from App state, so it can’t be
hoisted as in Reusing Elements. To make it work, it was rendered inside
Main, which has its own counter: “the &lt;Footer /&gt; gets rerendered every
time we click the count button even if the color doesn’t change.”
function Footer({ color }: { color: string }) {
	return &lt;footer style={{ color }}&gt;I am the ({color}) footer&lt;/footer&gt;
}

function Main({ color }: { color: string }) {
	const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
	const increment = () =&gt; setCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)
	return (
		&lt;div&gt;
			&lt;button onClick={increment}&gt;The count is {count}&lt;/button&gt;
			{/* 🐨 interpolate the footer here rather than creating a new &lt;Footer /&gt; element every render */}
			&lt;Footer color={color} /&gt;
		&lt;/div&gt;
	)
}

function App() {
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	return (
		&lt;div&gt;
			{/* ...color buttons... */}
			&lt;Main color={color} /&gt;
		&lt;/div&gt;
	)
}
Main doesn’t use color at all. It only forwards it, which is prop
drilling. And because &lt;Footer color={color} /&gt; lives in Main’s body,
every Main count update creates a new footer element.
The fix: build the element where its data lives, pass it down
function Main({ footer }: { footer: React.ReactNode }) {
	const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
	const increment = () =&gt; setCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)
	return (
		&lt;div&gt;
			&lt;button onClick={increment}&gt;The count is {count}&lt;/button&gt;
			{footer}
		&lt;/div&gt;
	)
}

function App() {
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	return (
		&lt;div&gt;
			&lt;div&gt;
				&lt;p&gt;Set the footer color:&lt;/p&gt;
				&lt;div style={{ display: &#039;flex&#039;, gap: 4 }}&gt;
					&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setColor(&#039;black&#039;)}&gt;Black&lt;/button&gt;
					&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setColor(&#039;blue&#039;)}&gt;Blue&lt;/button&gt;
					&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setColor(&#039;green&#039;)}&gt;Green&lt;/button&gt;
				&lt;/div&gt;
			&lt;/div&gt;
			&lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer color={color} /&gt;} /&gt;
		&lt;/div&gt;
	)
}
The workshop’s summary: “we can still make dynamic elements that are
positioned where we want them to be without allowing them to be
rerendered with every state change by passing them as props!”
Why it works: the objects across two renders
Clicking Main’s count button schedules an update on Main only.
App() is not called:
// App&#039;s last render created (once):
const F1 = { type: Footer, props: { color: &#039;black&#039; } /* P1 */ }
// and &lt;Main footer={F1} /&gt;

// Main render 1 → returns [button, props.footer] → F1
// Main render 2 → returns [button, props.footer] → F1  (same object: App didn&#039;t run)

P1 !== P1   // false → Footer skipped
Clicking “Blue” schedules an update on App:
// App render 2 creates a NEW element:
const F2 = { type: Footer, props: { color: &#039;blue&#039; } /* P2 */ }

P1 !== P2   // true → Footer() runs (correct: the color changed)
The same JSX, &lt;Footer color={color} /&gt;, gives different results purely
because of which function body it’s in.
Element identity follows the creator’s renders
This is the general rule the step teaches: an element is only as fresh
as the render that created it. Moving creation up from Main to App
ties the footer’s identity to App’s update frequency (color changes)
instead of Main’s (count changes). The two kinds of state now drive
re-renders independently.
Kent’s blog post
uses the same shape:
&lt;Counter logger={&lt;Logger label=&quot;counter&quot; /&gt;} /&gt;
The counter’s state changes never touch the Logger element, because the
counter didn’t create it. His recommendation is to move the element up to
a component that renders less often and pass it down, rather than
sprinkling memo. That’s structural, costs nothing at runtime, and often
reads better too.

💬 This is also why “layout” components that take children are cheap
to re-render. A &lt;Modal&gt; with open/close state doesn’t re-render its
children when it toggles, because those children were created by the
modal’s parent.

Element Props, step by step
// STEP 1: mount. React calls App(): color = &#039;black&#039;
//   creates F1 = &lt;Footer color=&quot;black&quot; /&gt;    (props P1)
//   creates &lt;Main footer={F1} /&gt;
// STEP 2: React calls Main({ footer: F1 }): count = 0
//   returns &lt;div&gt; with a NEW &lt;button&gt; and F1
// STEP 3: React calls Footer({ color: &#039;black&#039; }). Commit.

// STEP 4: user clicks Main&#039;s count button → setCount schedules an update on Main ONLY.

// STEP 5: React starts rendering at Main. App() is NOT called,
//   so nothing re-creates the footer element.
// STEP 6: Main() runs → a NEW &lt;button&gt; (&quot;The count is 1&quot;) plus props.footer → F1

// STEP 7: Footer slot: P1 !== P1 → false → BAIL OUT (Footer() not called)
// STEP 8: commit. Only the button text changes.

// Contrast: user clicks &quot;Blue&quot;
// STEP 9:  setColor(&#039;blue&#039;) → App() runs → creates F2 = &lt;Footer color=&quot;blue&quot; /&gt; (P2)
// STEP 10: &lt;Main footer={F2}/&gt; has new props → Main() runs → returns F2
// STEP 11: P1 !== P2 → true → Footer({ color: &#039;blue&#039; }) runs → DOM updates
Rules and caveats

It helps only when the receiver re-renders more often than the
creator. If both re-render together, the element is new every time.
One unrelated piece of state in the creator undoes it. Any App
update creates a new footer element. Context fixes that by removing
the dynamic prop entirely.
Type the slot as React.ReactNode. That’s what the workshop does,
and it lets callers pass any element, string, or null.
Prefer it to memo when it keeps the code clear. There’s no
comparison cost and no deps to keep in sync.

