Memoize Components

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Performance
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 1, “Element Optimization”, step 5
memo) plus the react.dev memo
reference and the React 19.2.5 source. Part of Element Optimization.
Previous step: Memoize Elements. My own notes and clarifications are
marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: How does React.memo decide whether to re-render?
A: It compares the previous and next props shallowly: same number of
keys, and each value Object.is-equal. If they’re equal, React skips the
component and reuses its last output. You can pass a custom
arePropsEqual(prev, next) as the second argument. In our example,
memo(FooterImpl) skips app-count clicks because { name: &#039;Kody&#039; } and
{ name: &#039;Kody&#039; } are shallowly equal, even though they’re different
objects.
Q: What commonly breaks memo without anyone noticing?
A: Props that are new references on every render: inline objects
(style={{…}}), arrays, arrow functions (onClick={() =&gt; …}), and JSX
children. One such prop makes the shallow comparison fail every time.
React then pays for the comparison and renders anyway. Stabilize those
props with useMemo/useCallback, hoist constants, or pass primitives.
Q: memo vs useMemo: what’s the difference, and when do you use which?
A: Both skip work when inputs are Object.is-equal.

useMemo caches a value (possibly an element) in the parent, keyed
by a deps array.
memo wraps a component and uses its props as the deps array,
wherever it’s rendered.

Use memo when a component is expensive and rendered from several places
or with frequently re-created elements. Use useMemo for expensive
calculations or to stabilize a value you pass to a memoized child. The
workshop: memo “effectively turns the props object into a dependency
array a la useMemo.”
Q: Does memo prevent re-renders from state or context?
A: No. memo only skips parent-driven renders with equal props. The
component still re-renders for its own state updates and for any context
it reads. In our example, FooterImpl still re-renders on color changes,
because it reads ColorContext.
Q: Does the React Compiler make memo obsolete?
A: Largely, for new code. With the compiler enabled, react.dev’s memo
page says it memoizes components and values automatically, so manual
memo/useMemo/useCallback are mostly unnecessary. Understanding
reference equality is still what lets you debug an unexpected re-render or
code that isn’t compiled.
The problem: an inline element with a dynamic prop
The workshop moves the Footer “out of useMemo”, so App creates it
inline again, and every App render re-renders Footer:
function Footer({ name }: { name: string }) {
	const color = useColor()
	return (
		&lt;footer style={{ color }}&gt;
			I am the ({color}) footer, {name || &#039;Unnamed&#039;}
		&lt;/footer&gt;
	)
}

// in App:
&lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer name={name} /&gt;} /&gt;
The goal: Footer renders only when name or color change, with no
memoization at the call site.
The fix: memoize the component itself
const Footer = memo(function FooterImpl({ name }: { name: string }) {
	const color = useColor()
	return (
		&lt;footer style={{ color }}&gt;
			I am the ({color}) footer, {name || &#039;Unnamed&#039;}
		&lt;/footer&gt;
	)
})
App doesn’t change. It still writes &lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer name={name} /&gt;} /&gt;.

💬 The inner function is named FooterImpl because the workshop’s tests
look for that name in the list of called components. A named inner
function also shows up properly in DevTools and stack traces.

Inside shallowEqual
When React reaches a memo component (updateMemoComponent in
react-dom-client), it picks the comparison function:
Component = Component.compare;
Component = null !== Component ? Component : shallowEqual;
and the default, verbatim from React 19.2.5:
function shallowEqual(objA, objB) {
  if (objectIs(objA, objB)) return !0;
  if (&quot;object&quot; !== typeof objA || null === objA ||
      &quot;object&quot; !== typeof objB || null === objB)
    return !1;
  var keysA = Object.keys(objA), keysB = Object.keys(objB);
  if (keysA.length !== keysB.length) return !1;
  for (keysB = 0; keysB &lt; keysA.length; keysB++) {
    var currentKey = keysA[keysB];
    if (!hasOwnProperty.call(objB, currentKey) ||
        !objectIs(objA[currentKey], objB[currentKey]))
      return !1;
  }
  return !0;
}
Read it top to bottom:

Same props object? Then they’re equal. This is the plain-component
check from Reusing Elements, kept as a fast path.
Same number of keys? Otherwise, not equal.
Every key Object.is-equal Otherwise, not equal.

It doesn’t recurse, which is what “shallow” means. { user: { name: &#039;Kody&#039; } } vs { user: { name: &#039;Kody&#039; } } compares user by reference,
and two separate objects fail.
Why it works: the objects across two renders
// App render 1
#A = { type: Footer /* memo */, props: { name: &#039;Kody&#039; } /* PA */ }
// App render 2 (app count click): a NEW element and a NEW props object
#B = { type: Footer /* memo */, props: { name: &#039;Kody&#039; } /* PB */ }

PA === PB                   // false → a plain component would render here
// memo → shallowEqual(PA, PB):
Object.is(PA, PB)           // false, keep going
keys: [&#039;name&#039;] vs [&#039;name&#039;]  // same length
Object.is(&#039;Kody&#039;, &#039;Kody&#039;)   // true → equal → BAIL OUT, FooterImpl not called

// typed &quot;Kodyy&quot; → PC = { name: &#039;Kodyy&#039; }
Object.is(&#039;Kody&#039;, &#039;Kodyy&#039;)  // false → FooterImpl runs
Compared with Memoize Elements: there the element stayed the same
and React’s identity check passed. Here the element is new every time, and
memo moves the check to prop values.
What breaks memo
Each ❌ below is a new reference on every App render, so shallowEqual
fails on that key even though name matches:
// ❌ new object
&lt;Footer name={name} style={{ fontWeight: &#039;bold&#039; }} /&gt;
// ❌ new function
&lt;Footer name={name} onClick={() =&gt; console.log(name)} /&gt;
// ❌ children is a new element
&lt;Footer name={name}&gt;&lt;span&gt;hi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/Footer&gt;

// ✅ stable versions
const bold = { fontWeight: &#039;bold&#039; }                     // module scope
const handleClick = useCallback(() =&gt; console.log(name), [name])
&lt;Footer name={name} style={bold} onClick={handleClick} /&gt;
The full prop-type table is in Element Optimization (“What breaks memo” section).
Where to render a memoized component
Kent’s closing aside: at this point Footer could be rendered “directly in
the Main” with the same behavior. The only difference is that React would
then run the props comparison on every Main count click, instead of the
cheaper element-identity check it gets from receiving props.footer. “In
practical scenarios, this shouldn’t really make much of a difference.” In
other words, after memo, placement is mostly a matter of taste.
Memoize Components, step by step
// STEP 0: module load: memo(FooterImpl) returns a memo component type `Footer`.
//         FooterImpl isn&#039;t called.

// STEP 1: mount. App() creates #A (props PA = { name: &#039;Kody&#039; })
//   Main() returns #A → React calls FooterImpl(PA) → subscribes to ColorContext

// STEP 2: click &quot;app count&quot; → App() runs → creates NEW #B (PB = { name: &#039;Kody&#039; })
//   Main() runs → returns #B
//   updateMemoComponent: compare = shallowEqual
//     Object.is(PA, PB) false → keys 1 vs 1 → Object.is(&#039;Kody&#039;,&#039;Kody&#039;) true
//   → BAIL OUT. Components called: App, Main (as the workshop test expects)

// STEP 3: click Main&#039;s own count → only Main() runs → props.footer is still #B
//   shallowEqual(PB, PB) → first line: Object.is(PB, PB) true → BAIL OUT

// STEP 4: type &quot;y&quot; → App() → #C (PC = { name: &#039;Kodyy&#039; })
//   shallowEqual: Object.is(&#039;Kody&#039;,&#039;Kodyy&#039;) false → FooterImpl(PC) runs

// STEP 5: click &quot;Blue&quot; → App() → #D, props equal to PC
//   shallowEqual → true, BUT the fiber is marked by context propagation
//   → FooterImpl runs with the new color (&quot;footer should re-render when color changes&quot;)
Rules and caveats

memo is only as good as its least stable prop. Audit call sites
for inline objects, arrays, functions, and JSX children, or it’s pure
overhead.
A custom arePropsEqual must compare every prop, including
functions. Skipping one can leave a stale closure rendering old data.
Deep equality checks can cost more than the render they avoid.
Measure before and after. If the component is cheap, or its props
change on most renders anyway, memo only adds a comparison.
Prefer structure when it’s clear. Reusing Elements,
Element Props and Context cost nothing at runtime. The workshop’s
closing reminder puts human readability first unless there’s a real
performance problem.

