Memoize Elements

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Performance
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 1, “Element Optimization”, step 4
use-memo) plus the react.dev useMemo
reference. Part of Element Optimization. Previous step: Context.
Next step: Memoize Components. My own notes and clarifications are
marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: How does useMemo decide whether to recompute?
A: It stores the last result and the last deps array on the component’s
fiber. On each render it compares every new dependency with the old one at
the same position using Object.is. If all match, it returns the stored
reference without calling the factory. Otherwise it calls the factory and
stores the new result. In our example:
useMemo(() =&gt; &lt;Footer name={name} /&gt;, [name]) returns the same element
until name changes.
Q: What happens when a dependency is an object or a function created during render?
A: Object.is compares objects by reference, so a dependency created
inline ({ ... }, [...], () =&gt; …) is “changed” on every render. The
memo recomputes every time, and you pay for the comparison with no benefit.
Dependencies need to be primitives or stable references (state values,
refs, or other memoized values).
Q: Can you useMemo a JSX element? Why would you?
A: Yes. An element is just an object, so it can be cached like any value.
Returning the same element object lets React’s cheapest check (same props
object → skip) apply even to an element with dynamic props. Kent calls
this “a bit of an unconventional approach”. Memoize Components is the
conventional tool for the same job.
Q: Is useMemo a guarantee?
A: No. react.dev describes it as a performance optimization. React may
discard the cache (for example, during development or in the future), so
code must stay correct if the factory re-runs. Never rely on it for
semantics, such as “this effect only fires once because the value is
memoized.”
The problem: a dynamic prop that must stay a prop
The footer now shows a user-entered name, and the workshop requires it
to be a regular prop: “we don’t want to use context for the name like we
do with the color just yet.” A prop that depends on state means the element
can’t be hoisted (Reusing Elements) and can’t be routed through context
(Context):
function Footer({ name }: { name: string }) {
	const color = useColor()
	return (
		&lt;footer style={{ color }}&gt;
			I am the ({color}) footer, {name || &#039;Unnamed&#039;}
		&lt;/footer&gt;
	)
}

function App() {
	const [appCount, setAppCount] = useState(0)
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [name, setName] = useState(&#039;Kody&#039;)
	// 🐨 memoize the footer variable here with useMemo
	const footer = &lt;Footer name={name} /&gt;
	// ...
}
Every App render (an app-count click, a color change, a keystroke)
creates a new footer element.
The fix: cache the element by its dependencies
The workshop’s hint: “Remember that useMemo allows you to get the same
value back if the dependencies haven’t changed. Well, you can create a
React element that depends on the name and render that”:
function App() {
	const [appCount, setAppCount] = useState(0)
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [name, setName] = useState(&#039;Kody&#039;)
	const footer = useMemo(() =&gt; &lt;Footer name={name} /&gt;, [name])
	return (
		&lt;ColorContext value={color}&gt;
			{/* ...color buttons, name input... */}
			&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setAppCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)}&gt;
				The app count is {appCount}
			&lt;/button&gt;
			&lt;Main footer={footer} /&gt;
		&lt;/ColorContext&gt;
	)
}
Think of the deps array as the full list of things that could make the
element different. The footer element contains only name, so [name]
is complete. appCount and Main’s count aren’t in it, so they reuse the
cached element. color isn’t in it either, but Footer reads color
through context, so it still updates (see Context).
What useMemo actually stores
In simplified pseudocode (not React’s real code), useMemo is:
function useMemo(factory, deps) {
  const hook = getHookForThisCall()              // per-component, per-call-order slot
  if (hook.deps !== undefined &amp;&amp;
      deps.every((d, i) =&gt; Object.is(d, hook.deps[i]))) {
    return hook.value                            // SAME reference as last render
  }
  hook.value = factory()                         // NEW reference
  hook.deps = deps
  return hook.value
}
Two properties matter here:

The comparison is on the deps, not on the result. React never checks
whether the new element “looks the same”.
A match returns the old object itself, not a copy. That’s what makes
the downstream props !== props check fail and React bail out.

Why it works: the objects across three renders
// render 1 (mount): no cache → factory runs
hook = { deps: [&#039;Kody&#039;], value: #A }
#A = { type: Footer, props: { name: &#039;Kody&#039; } /* P1 */ }

// render 2 (app count click)
Object.is(&#039;Kody&#039;, &#039;Kody&#039;)   // true → return #A, factory NOT called
Footer slot: P1 !== P1      // false → BAIL OUT

// render 3 (typed &quot;Kodyy&quot;)
Object.is(&#039;Kody&#039;, &#039;Kodyy&#039;)  // false → factory runs
hook = { deps: [&#039;Kodyy&#039;], value: #B }
#B = { type: Footer, props: { name: &#039;Kodyy&#039; } /* P2 */ }
Footer slot: P1 !== P2      // true → Footer() runs (correct)
The object-dependency trap, as a counter-example:
// ❌ user is a NEW object every App render
const user = { name }
const footer = useMemo(() =&gt; &lt;Footer user={user} /&gt;, [user])
// Object.is(prevUser, nextUser) → false EVERY render → new element every time

// ✅ depend on the primitive (or keep `user` in state and replace it immutably)
const footer = useMemo(() =&gt; &lt;Footer name={name} /&gt;, [name])
Memoize Elements, step by step
// STEP 1: mount. App(): name=&#039;Kody&#039;
//   useMemo: no cache → call factory → #A; store deps [&#039;Kody&#039;]
//   Main() returns #A → Footer({ name: &#039;Kody&#039; }) runs, subscribes to ColorContext

// STEP 2: click &quot;app count&quot; → App() runs
//   useMemo: Object.is(&#039;Kody&#039;,&#039;Kody&#039;) → return #A (factory skipped)
//   provider value unchanged
//   Main() runs (new props literal) → returns #A
//   Footer: P1 !== P1 false → BAIL OUT

// STEP 3: type &quot;y&quot; → setName(&#039;Kodyy&#039;) → App() runs
//   useMemo: Object.is(&#039;Kody&#039;,&#039;Kodyy&#039;) false → factory → #B; store [&#039;Kodyy&#039;]
//   Main() returns #B → P1 !== P2 → Footer({ name: &#039;Kodyy&#039; }) runs

// STEP 4: click &quot;Blue&quot; → App() runs
//   useMemo: deps [&#039;Kodyy&#039;] unchanged → return #B
//   provider: Object.is(&#039;black&#039;,&#039;blue&#039;) false → Footer marked
//   Footer: props identical, but marked → Footer() runs with new color, same name
Rules and caveats

List every value the factory reads. A missing dependency gives a
stale element, for example a footer that keeps showing an old name.
Lint with react-hooks/exhaustive-deps.
Primitives or stable references only. One inline object or function
in the deps makes the memo recompute on every render.
The optimization lives at the call site. Every place that renders
Footer needs its own useMemo. memo puts it on the component once,
which is why the workshop follows with Memoize Components.
Not a semantic guarantee. Correctness must not depend on the
factory being skipped.

