Reusing Elements

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Performance
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 1, “Element Optimization”, step 1
reuse) plus his post One simple trick to optimize React re-renders.
Part of Element Optimization, which covers the shared mental model:
element objects, Object.is, and React’s bail-out check. Next step:
Element Props. My own notes and clarifications are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why does a child component re-render when its parent re-renders, even if its props didn’t change?
A: Re-rendering a parent means calling the parent function again, and every
JSX expression in it runs again. &lt;Child /&gt; is jsx(Child, {}), which
returns a new element with a new props object each time. React’s
default check for a plain component is “is this the same props object as
last time?”, and a new object never is. So React calls the child. It
can’t know the output would be identical without running it. In our
example, every click on the counter button re-renders App, which creates
a new &lt;Footer /&gt; element, so Footer re-renders too.
Q: If you create an element once and reuse it, why does React skip re-rendering it?
A: Because the element, and therefore its props object, is the same
reference as last render. React relies on components being idempotent
(same props in, same output out), so rendering again with the identical
props object would be pointless. Kent’s rule: “If you give React the same
element you gave it on the last render, it won’t bother re-rendering that
element.” In our example, the fix is one line:
const footer = &lt;Footer /&gt;   // created once, at module load
Q: When can’t you hoist an element out of a component?
A: When any of its props come from the component’s scope (state, props,
anything computed during render). A module-level element runs once and
can’t see those values. Structural workarounds come next: pass the
element in from a component that re-renders less often
(Element Props), or deliver the dynamic value through context
(Context). useMemo (Memoize Elements) or memo
(Memoize Components) cover the rest.
Q: Does reusing an element mean the component never re-renders?
A: No. It only removes re-renders caused by the parent. The component
still re-renders when its own state changes or when a context it reads
changes. In our example Footer has neither, so it renders exactly once.
The problem: an element created inside render is new every render
The workshop’s setup: “Right now, when you click the counter button, both
the App and Footer components rerender. But the Footer component
doesn’t change so that render is unnecessary.”
function Footer() {
	return &lt;footer&gt;I am the footer&lt;/footer&gt;
}

function App() {
	const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
	const increment = () =&gt; setCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)
	return (
		&lt;div&gt;
			&lt;button onClick={increment}&gt;The count is {count}&lt;/button&gt;
			{/* 🐨 change this to an interpolation and interpolate the footer */}
			&lt;Footer /&gt;
		&lt;/div&gt;
	)
}
Nothing about Footer depends on count, but &lt;Footer /&gt; sits inside
App’s function body. Every setCount call re-runs that body, and the JSX
line runs with it.
The fix: create the element once, outside the component
function Footer() {
	return &lt;footer&gt;I am the footer&lt;/footer&gt;
}

const footer = &lt;Footer /&gt;

function App() {
	const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
	const increment = () =&gt; setCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)
	return (
		&lt;div&gt;
			&lt;button onClick={increment}&gt;The count is {count}&lt;/button&gt;
			{footer}
		&lt;/div&gt;
	)
}
Kent’s explanation: “When React is given the same element it was given on
the last render, it won’t bother rerendering that element again because
that would be pointless. It just keeps the same element and moves on.”
Why it works: the objects across two renders
Write the elements out as the plain objects they are (see
Element Optimization &gt; What a React element actually is). #A, #B
label distinct object identities.
Before (inline &lt;Footer /&gt;):
// App render 1 (mount)
{ type: Footer, props: {} /* P1 */ }   // element #A

// App render 2 (after click): jsx(Footer, {}) runs AGAIN
{ type: Footer, props: {} /* P2 */ }   // element #B

// React&#039;s check for the Footer slot:
P1 !== P2   // true: two different {} objects → call Footer()
After (hoisted footer):
// module load, once:
const footer = { type: Footer, props: {} /* P1 */ }   // element #A

// App render 1: {footer} → #A
// App render 2: {footer} → #A  (App just reads the variable)

P1 !== P1   // false: same object → bail out, Footer() not called

💬 Both “before” props objects print as {}. They look identical, and
that’s the trap. {} === {} is false. React never looks inside the
props object. It only asks “is this the same object?”, and === on
objects compares the pointer, not the contents.

The check itself is one line in React’s beginWork
(react-dom-client.development.js, React 19.2.5):
current.memoizedProps !== workInProgress.pendingProps ||
memoizedProps is what the fiber rendered with last time. pendingProps
is the new element’s props. Only when they’re the same object and the
fiber has no pending state or context update does React skip the call.
Why React is allowed to skip: idempotent components
Kent’s blog post
runs the same experiment with a Logger that console.logs on every
render. Created inline, it logs on every click. Created once and passed in,
it logs once “and then never again”. The reason React may skip is a
contract: components are expected to be pure functions of their props (and
state and context). If the props object is the very same object, a pure
component would produce the very same output, so running it again would
be wasted work. This is also why side effects belong in effects and event
handlers, not in the render body: a render React decided to skip never
runs.
Reusing Elements, step by step
One click on the counter, with the hoisted solution:
// STEP 0: module evaluation. Runs once.
const footer = &lt;Footer /&gt;        // jsx(Footer, {}) → element #A, props P1

// STEP 1: mount. React calls App().
//   useState → count = 0; new `increment` closure
//   returns &lt;div&gt; with a NEW &lt;button&gt; element and #A
// STEP 2: React calls Footer() for the first time and commits the DOM.

// STEP 3: user clicks → setCount(c =&gt; c + 1) schedules an update on App.

// STEP 4: React calls App() again.
//   count = 1; a NEW &lt;div&gt;, a NEW &lt;button&gt; (&quot;The count is 1&quot;)
//   {footer} evaluates to #A again (nothing re-created it)

// STEP 5: reconciling the &lt;button&gt;: new props → update its text node.

// STEP 6: reconciling the Footer slot:
//   current.memoizedProps (P1) !== pendingProps (P1)  → false
//   no pending state, no context read               → BAIL OUT
//   Footer() is NOT called; its previous output is reused

// STEP 7: commit. Only the button&#039;s text node changes in the DOM.
Rules and caveats

Only static elements can be hoisted. The moment a prop depends on
state, the element has to be created during render. Then you restructure
or memoize.
A shared element is fine. Elements are immutable descriptions, not
component instances. Rendering the same footer in two places gives two
separate Footer instances, each with its own state.
This isn’t a license to hoist everything. The workshop is explicit
that this exercise is practice for when it’s needed, and it closes with:
“focus more of your efforts on making it easier for humans to understand
your code than on making it faster for the computer to run unless you
have a specific performance problem.”
Measure it. Use React DevTools’ “Highlight updates when components
render” or the Profiler before and after. The workshop asks for exactly
this, and its tests assert which components were called.

Sources

