Kent C. Dodds: One simple trick to optimize React re-renders
React 19.2.5 source: react-dom/cjs/react-dom-client.development.js (beginWork)

Memoize Context

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Performance
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 2, “Optimize Context”, step 1
memo-context) plus the react.dev useContext
reference. Part of Optimize Context. Next step: Provider Component.
Relies on Memoize Elements for how useMemo compares deps. My own notes
and clarifications are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why memoize a context provider’s value?
A: React decides whether consumers must update by comparing the old and
new value with Object.is. An object or array built during render is a
new reference every time, so every consumer re-renders on every render of
the provider’s owner, even when the data is the same. useMemo returns
the same reference until the real data changes. In our example:
const value = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ color, name }), [color, name])
Q: Why doesn’t memo on the consumer fix this by itself?
A: memo compares props. A context change bypasses that comparison
entirely. The consumer re-renders because the context changed, not
because of its props. In our example Footer was already memo-ized, and
it still re-rendered on every app-count click until the value was
memoized.
Q: What goes in the dependency array for a context value?
A: Every reactive value inside the object. You can leave out values React
guarantees to be stable (useState setters, useReducer dispatch, refs),
though listing them is harmless. Functions you define yourself need
useCallback first, or the memo recomputes every render. In our example,
[color, name] is complete.
Q: After memoizing, which components still re-render on an unrelated state change?
A: The ones that re-render because their parent did. Memoizing the value
only removes the context-driven re-render. In our example, an app-count
click still renders App, FooterSetters and Main, just not Footer.
Provider Component tackles the rest.
The problem: a new context value on every render
The workshop combined name and color into one FooterContext and
pulled the controls into a FooterSetters component. Then: “if you pull up
the React DevTools, you’ll notice all the components rerender whenever you
change the counter in the App component. This is happening because the
FooterContext is a new value prop every render.”
const FooterContext = createContext&lt;{
	color: string
	name: string
} | null&gt;(null)

const Footer = memo(function FooterImpl() {
	const { color, name } = useFooter()
	return (
		&lt;footer style={{ color }}&gt;
			I am the ({color}) footer, {name || &#039;Unnamed&#039;}
		&lt;/footer&gt;
	)
})

function App() {
	const [appCount, setAppCount] = useState(0)
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [name, setName] = useState(&#039;&#039;)
	// 🐨 wrap this in useMemo
	const value = { color, name }
	return (
		&lt;FooterContext value={value}&gt;
			&lt;div&gt;
				&lt;FooterSetters setName={setName} setColor={setColor} /&gt;
				&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setAppCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)}&gt;
					The app count is {appCount}
				&lt;/button&gt;
				&lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer /&gt;} /&gt;
			&lt;/div&gt;
		&lt;/FooterContext&gt;
	)
}
Footer is memoized and its props are always {}, so memo’s
shallowEqual passes on every render. The re-render comes from the other
path: the context.
The fix: memoize the value
function App() {
	const [appCount, setAppCount] = useState(0)
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [name, setName] = useState(&#039;&#039;)
	const value = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ color, name }), [color, name])
	return (
		&lt;FooterContext value={value}&gt;
			&lt;div&gt;
				&lt;FooterSetters setName={setName} setColor={setColor} /&gt;
				&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setAppCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)}&gt;
					The app count is {appCount}
				&lt;/button&gt;
				&lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer /&gt;} /&gt;
			&lt;/div&gt;
		&lt;/FooterContext&gt;
	)
}
The workshop’s intro sums up the effect: “By memoizing the value, you’re
ensuring that the value is only re-created when the count value changes.
As a result, the consuming components will only rerender when the count
value changes.”
Why it works: the value objects across two renders
Before, on an app-count click:
// App render 1: value = { color: &#039;black&#039;, name: &#039;&#039; }   /* V1 */
// App render 2: value = { color: &#039;black&#039;, name: &#039;&#039; }   /* V2: new literal */

// &lt;Footer /&gt; is a new element, but memo → shallowEqual({}, {}) → true (props OK)
// provider: Object.is(V1, V2) → false → Footer&#039;s fiber is marked
// → FooterImpl runs anyway
After:
// App render 1: useMemo → V1, deps [&#039;black&#039;, &#039;&#039;]
// App render 2: deps [&#039;black&#039;, &#039;&#039;] vs [&#039;black&#039;, &#039;&#039;] → return V1
// provider: Object.is(V1, V1) → true → no propagation
// memo: shallowEqual({}, {}) → true → FooterImpl skipped
Color change (after):
// deps [&#039;black&#039;, &#039;&#039;] vs [&#039;blue&#039;, &#039;&#039;] → factory runs → V3
// Object.is(V1, V3) → false → Footer marked → FooterImpl runs (correct)
Two independent gates now guard Footer, and both must pass for it to
skip:




















GateChecksProtects againstmemoshallowEqual(prevProps, nextProps)the parent re-renderingmemoized context valueObject.is(prevValue, nextValue)the provider’s owner re-rendering
What memoizing the value doesn’t fix
The workshop test for this step expects an app-count click to render
exactly [&#039;App&#039;, &#039;FooterSetters&#039;, &#039;Main&#039;]:

App owns appCount.
FooterSetters is a plain component rendered by App. It gets a new
props object ({ setName, setColor } literal) every time App renders.
Its props are equal but it’s not memo-ized, so React doesn’t compare
them.
Main gets a new footer element prop.

A color change is worse: it re-renders App and Main too, even though
neither reads the footer state. The state lives in App, so the whole
App subtree re-renders. That’s the problem Provider Component solves
by moving the state out of App.
Memoize Context, step by step
// STEP 1: mount. App(): appCount=0, color=&#039;black&#039;, name=&#039;&#039;
//   useMemo → V1 = { color: &#039;black&#039;, name: &#039;&#039; }, deps stored
//   FooterSetters, Main, FooterImpl render. FooterImpl reads FooterContext.

// STEP 2: click &quot;The app count is 0&quot; → setAppCount → update on App
// STEP 3: App() runs. useMemo deps unchanged → V1
// STEP 4: provider: Object.is(V1, V1) → true → no consumers marked
// STEP 5: FooterSetters() runs (plain component, new props object)
// STEP 6: Main() runs (new footer element prop)
// STEP 7: &lt;Footer/&gt; slot: memo → shallowEqual({}, {}) true, not marked → BAIL OUT
//         Rendered: App, FooterSetters, Main (the workshop test&#039;s expectation)

// STEP 8: click &quot;Blue&quot; → setColor(&#039;blue&#039;) → App() runs
// STEP 9: useMemo deps [&#039;black&#039;,&#039;&#039;] → [&#039;blue&#039;,&#039;&#039;] changed → V2
// STEP 10: provider: Object.is(V1, V2) false → FooterImpl marked
// STEP 11: FooterSetters, Main re-render (parent), FooterImpl re-renders (context)
Rules and caveats

Every object or array value needs useMemo. Inline value={{…}} or
value={[…]} defeats memo on every consumer.
Functions in the value need useCallback, otherwise the useMemo
deps change every render. react.dev’s useContext page shows exactly
this login + currentUser combination.
Memoizing the value is necessary but not sufficient. Consumers that
also re-render from their parent (like FooterSetters here) aren’t
helped.
The deps array must be complete. If you leave out name, the footer
shows a stale name. The value is a cache and must be invalidated
correctly.

