Provider Component

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Performance
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 2, “Optimize Context”, step 2
provider-component) plus his post How to use React Context effectively.
Part of Optimize Context. Previous step: Memoize Context. Next
step: Split Context. The mechanism is the element reuse from
Element Props, applied to children. My own notes and clarifications
are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why put context state in a dedicated provider component that accepts children?
A: If the state lives in a big parent (like App), every state change
re-renders that parent and its whole subtree, even components that don’t
use the context. In a XProvider({ children }) component, a state change
re-renders only the provider. children is the same element object that
was created by the provider’s parent, so React bails out of the subtree
and only the context consumers re-render. In our example, changing the
footer color no longer re-renders App or Main.
Q: Why doesn’t re-rendering FooterProvider re-render its children?
A: children is a prop, created by whoever rendered
&lt;FooterProvider&gt;…&lt;/FooterProvider&gt;, which is App. When only
FooterProvider’s state changes, App doesn’t run, so props.children
is the same object as last time, and React’s identity check skips it. It’s
the same rule as Element Props: an element is only as fresh as the
render that created it.
Q: Is this pattern common?
A: Very. It’s how practically every context-based library and app
structures providers (ThemeProvider, AuthProvider, …). The workshop’s
owl: “It’s kind of natural. It just means that people are benefiting from
the optimizations that React has without thinking about it!” Kent’s
“How to use React Context effectively” post recommends exactly this
provider-component-plus-custom-hook shape.
Q: What still re-renders after this change?
A: Every consumer of the context, including those that only use the
setters. In our example FooterSetters re-renders on every color or name
change, even though setters never change. Split Context fixes that.
The problem: context state lives in the top-level component
After Memoize Context, color and name still live in App. The
workshop: “when we change some of the Footer context stuff, we’re actually
rerendering the App and Main components even though those don’t have
anything to do with the Footer context.”
function App() {
	const [appCount, setAppCount] = useState(0)
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [name, setName] = useState(&#039;&#039;)
	const value = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ color, name }), [color, name])
	return (
		&lt;FooterContext value={value}&gt;
			&lt;div&gt;
				&lt;FooterSetters setName={setName} setColor={setColor} /&gt;
				{/* ... */}
				&lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer /&gt;} /&gt;
			&lt;/div&gt;
		&lt;/FooterContext&gt;
	)
}
A setColor call re-renders App itself. Every element in its body
(&lt;div&gt;, &lt;FooterSetters&gt;, &lt;Main&gt;, &lt;Footer&gt;) is recreated, and the
whole tree re-renders.
The fix: a provider component that owns the state
const FooterContext = createContext&lt;{
	color: string
	setColor: (color: string) =&gt; void
	name: string
	setName: (name: string) =&gt; void
} | null&gt;(null)

function FooterProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [name, setName] = useState(&#039;&#039;)
	const value = useMemo(
		() =&gt; ({ color, setColor, name, setName }),
		[color, name],
	)
	return &lt;FooterContext value={value}&gt;{children}&lt;/FooterContext&gt;
}

function FooterSetters() {
	const { setColor, setName } = useFooter()
	// ...same color buttons and name input...
}

function App() {
	const [appCount, setAppCount] = useState(0)
	return (
		&lt;FooterProvider&gt;
			&lt;div&gt;
				&lt;FooterSetters /&gt;
				&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setAppCount((c) =&gt; c + 1)}&gt;
					The app count is {appCount}
				&lt;/button&gt;
				&lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer /&gt;} /&gt;
			&lt;/div&gt;
		&lt;/FooterProvider&gt;
	)
}
Three things moved:

The state moved from App into FooterProvider.
The setters moved into the context. FooterSetters now reads them
with useFooter() instead of taking props.
App only keeps appCount, which is its own concern.


💬 setColor/setName aren’t in the useMemo deps. useState setters
are guaranteed stable, so leaving them out is safe.

Why it works: children is the same object
Color click, where setColor lives in FooterProvider:
// App&#039;s last render created (once):
const C1 = &lt;div&gt;…&lt;FooterSetters/&gt;…&lt;Main footer={&lt;Footer/&gt;}/&gt;&lt;/div&gt;   // children
// → &lt;FooterProvider children={C1} /&gt;

// FooterProvider render 2 (color → &#039;blue&#039;):
//   value: useMemo deps [&#039;black&#039;,&#039;&#039;] → [&#039;blue&#039;,&#039;&#039;] → NEW V2
//   returns &lt;FooterContext value={V2}&gt;{props.children}&lt;/FooterContext&gt;
//   props.children is still C1: App did NOT run

// React reconciles C1 against C1 → same props object → BAIL OUT on the &lt;div&gt; subtree
// ...but the provider value changed: Object.is(V1, V2) false
// → mark consumers inside: FooterSetters (useFooter), FooterImpl (useFooter)
So React skips the &lt;div&gt; subtree as a whole and descends only to the
marked fibers. App and Main never run. That matches the workshop test:
the color click renders FooterProvider, FooterSetters and FooterImpl,
with App and Main explicitly not rendered.
App-count click, where setAppCount still lives in App:
// App() runs → creates NEW children C2 → &lt;FooterProvider children={C2}/&gt;
// FooterProvider() runs (new props) → useMemo deps unchanged → V1
// Object.is(V1, V1) true → no consumers marked
// FooterSetters: plain component, new element → renders
// Main: new props → renders; Footer: memo + unchanged context → skipped
// Test: [&#039;App&#039;, &#039;FooterProvider&#039;, &#039;FooterSetters&#039;, &#039;Main&#039;]
State colocation as a performance tool
The general lesson is put state as close as possible to the components
that use it. This step is state colocation for context. Moving color
and name down into FooterProvider means their updates start rendering
from FooterProvider instead of from App. React only re-renders from
the component whose state changed, downward, and element reuse cuts off
any subtree that was created higher up.
It also follows Kent’s recommended context structure from
How to use React Context effectively:

a XProvider component that owns the state and renders the context
a useX() hook that reads it and throws a helpful error outside the
provider

The workshop’s useFooter is exactly that hook:
function useFooter() {
	const context = use(FooterContext)
	if (!context) throw new Error(&#039;FooterContext not found&#039;)
	return context
}
Provider Component, step by step
// STEP 1: mount. App() creates children C1, renders &lt;FooterProvider&gt;{C1}
// STEP 2: FooterProvider(): color=&#039;black&#039;, name=&#039;&#039; → V1 (memoized)
// STEP 3: C1 renders: FooterSetters (reads context), Main, FooterImpl (reads context)

// STEP 4: click &quot;Blue&quot; → setColor lives in FooterProvider → update on FooterProvider ONLY
// STEP 5: FooterProvider() runs → useMemo deps changed → V2
// STEP 6: returns &lt;FooterContext value={V2}&gt;{C1}&lt;/FooterContext&gt;, same C1
// STEP 7: provider: Object.is(V1, V2) false → mark FooterSetters + FooterImpl
// STEP 8: C1 subtree: same props object → bail out, descend only to marked fibers
// STEP 9: FooterSetters() runs (context), FooterImpl() runs (context)
//         App() and Main() are NOT called
Rules and caveats

The provider must take children (or other element props) for this
to work. A provider that renders its own tree inline recreates it on
every state change.
Consumers still re-render on every value change, including
setter-only consumers like FooterSetters. That’s the next step.
Keep useMemo on the value. The provider component still
re-renders when its parent re-renders (the app-count click), and
without memoization that would produce a new value.
Keep the context private. Export the provider and the hook, not the
context object, so every consumer goes through the checked hook.

Sources

EpicWeb React Performance workshop, exercise 02.context (step provider-component, provider.test.ts)
Kent C. Dodds: How to use React Context effectively

Split Context

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Performance
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 2, “Optimize Context”, step 3
split-context) plus his post How to optimize your context value.
Part of Optimize Context. Previous step: Provider Component. My own
notes and clarifications are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What does “splitting context” mean, and when does it help?
A: Instead of one context holding both state and the functions that update
it, you use two: a state context that changes often and a
dispatch/setters context that never changes. Components that only
update state (forms, buttons, toolbars) read just the setters context,
so state changes never wake them. It helps when many or expensive
components only need the setters. In our example, FooterSetters stops
re-rendering on every color or name change.
Q: Why does the setters context never change?
A: useState setters and useReducer’s dispatch have stable
identity for the component’s lifetime. An object built only from them
and memoized with [] deps is created once. Its provider value is
Object.is-equal forever, so its consumers are never notified. In our
example:
const footerDispatchValue = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ setColor, setName }), [])
Q: Why also wrap the setter-only component in memo?
A: The split stops context re-renders, but the component can still
re-render because its parent did. With memo and no props, parent
renders are skipped too, so the component effectively never re-renders.
In our example, an app-count click no longer renders FooterSetters.
Q: What’s the cost of splitting?
A: API complexity: two providers, two hooks, and consumers that need both
have to call both. Kent’s verdict: “I personally feel like this is more
complicated of an API than is necessary for most situations.” Also, if
you wrap both contexts back into a single useFooter() hook that reads
both, you lose the benefit, since every caller subscribes to the state
context again.
Q: How does this relate to useReducer?
A: It’s the classic pairing: const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(…),
then state goes in one context and dispatch in another. dispatch is
stable, so it needs no useMemo at all. Kent’s post recommends exactly
this as “the simplest solution to optimizing your context value.”
The problem: setter-only consumers re-render on every state change
After Provider Component, one context holds everything:
const value = useMemo(
	() =&gt; ({ color, setColor, name, setName }),
	[color, name],
)
The workshop: “the FooterSetters component is rerendering whenever the
footer state changes, but that component doesn’t actually depend on the
footer state at all. All it cares about is the setter functions which
never change!” Destructuring { setColor, setName } doesn’t help. Reading
the context subscribes the whole component to the whole value.
Whenever color or name changes, the value object changes, and
FooterSetters re-renders.
The fix: one context for state, one for setters
const FooterStateContext = createContext&lt;{
	color: string
	name: string
} | null&gt;(null)
FooterStateContext.displayName = &#039;FooterStateContext&#039;

const FooterDispatchContext = createContext&lt;{
	setColor: (color: string) =&gt; void
	setName: (name: string) =&gt; void
} | null&gt;({
	setColor: () =&gt; {},
	setName: () =&gt; {},
})
FooterDispatchContext.displayName = &#039;FooterDispatchContext&#039;

function FooterProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
	const [color, setColor] = useState(&#039;black&#039;)
	const [name, setName] = useState(&#039;&#039;)
	const footerStateValue = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ color, name }), [color, name])
	const footerDispatchValue = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ setColor, setName }), [])
	return (
		&lt;FooterStateContext value={footerStateValue}&gt;
			&lt;FooterDispatchContext value={footerDispatchValue}&gt;
				{children}
			&lt;/FooterDispatchContext&gt;
		&lt;/FooterStateContext&gt;
	)
}

function useFooterState() {
	const context = use(FooterStateContext)
	if (!context) throw new Error(&#039;FooterStateContext not found&#039;)
	return context
}

function useFooterDispatch() {
	const context = use(FooterDispatchContext)
	if (!context) throw new Error(&#039;FooterDispatchContext not found&#039;)
	return context
}

const Footer = memo(function FooterImpl() {
	const { color, name } = useFooterState()
	// ...
})

const FooterSetters = memo(function FooterImplSetters() {
	const { setColor, setName } = useFooterDispatch()
	// ...same color buttons and name input...
})
App doesn’t change from the previous step. The extra credit (“memo-ize
the FooterSetters component and it will never rerender!”) is included
above.
Why it works: two values, two lifetimes
Color click:
// FooterProvider render 2:
//   footerStateValue:    deps [&#039;black&#039;,&#039;&#039;] → [&#039;blue&#039;,&#039;&#039;]  → NEW S2
//   footerDispatchValue: deps [] → [] (always equal)      → SAME D1

// FooterStateContext:    Object.is(S1, S2) false → mark FooterImpl
// FooterDispatchContext: Object.is(D1, D1) true  → mark nobody
// children: same element (App didn&#039;t run) → bail out
// → only FooterProvider and FooterImpl run. FooterSetters is NOT rendered
//   (the test: expect(componentNames).not.toContain(&#039;FooterSetters&#039;))
App-count click:
// App() runs → new children → FooterProvider() runs
//   S1 unchanged (deps equal), D1 unchanged
// FooterSetters: new element &lt;FooterSetters/&gt;, but memo → shallowEqual({}, {}) true,
//   not marked by context → BAIL OUT
// Main: renders (new footer prop). Footer: memo + no context change → skipped
FooterSetters now has no reason to re-render: no props (memo),
a dispatch context that never changes, and no state of its own. The
workshop’s test checks that it renders zero times during an app-count
click.
Split by update frequency
The deeper rule: one context per update frequency. A context
re-renders every consumer whenever any part of its value changes, so
bundling a fast-changing value with a never-changing one ties the
never-changing consumers to the fast-changing updates. Splitting lets each
consumer subscribe only to what it reads.
The same idea scales beyond state vs setters. A large context can be
split into slices (for example, user vs preferences vs
notifications) when their consumers are disjoint and the updates are
frequent. Past that point, a store with selector-based subscriptions
(useSyncExternalStore, Zustand, Redux) is often the better tool. Context
has no built-in “subscribe to part of the value”.

💬 displayName (the workshop’s 💯 tip) only affects React DevTools.
Without it, both providers show up as Context and are hard to tell
apart.
💬 The workshop gives FooterDispatchContext a no-op default value
instead of null. So useFooterDispatch() outside a provider returns the
no-ops instead of throwing, and its if (!context) check never fires. It
works, but it can hide a missing provider (buttons silently do nothing).
Use a null default if you want the error.

Split Context, step by step
// STEP 1: mount. FooterProvider(): S1 = {color:&#039;black&#039;, name:&#039;&#039;}, D1 = {setColor, setName}
//   FooterImplSetters reads FooterDispatchContext (D1)
//   FooterImpl reads FooterStateContext (S1)

// STEP 2: type &quot;K&quot; in the name input → setName(&#039;K&#039;) (from D1) → update on FooterProvider
// STEP 3: FooterProvider() runs
//   footerStateValue: [&#039;black&#039;,&#039;&#039;] → [&#039;black&#039;,&#039;K&#039;] changed → S2
//   footerDispatchValue: [] → reuse D1
// STEP 4: FooterStateContext: Object.is(S1,S2) false → mark FooterImpl
//         FooterDispatchContext: Object.is(D1,D1) true → nothing
// STEP 5: children (from App) unchanged → bail out, descend to marked fibers only
// STEP 6: FooterImpl() runs → &quot;I am the (black) footer, K&quot;
//         FooterImplSetters: not marked, props unchanged → NOT called
Rules and caveats

Consumers must read only the context they need. A convenience hook
that reads both contexts brings back the problem.
Memoize the dispatch value with [] only when everything in it is
truly stable (state setters, dispatch, refs, or useCallback([])
functions). A function that closes over state belongs with the state.
Pair it with memo on setter-only components, or they still
re-render with their parent.
Reach for it rarely. It’s worth it when many or expensive components
only update state. The workshop’s owl calls it “not very common (and as
is the case with most of these optimizations not normally necessary).”

