Next: 04 Tool schemas

claude/05-tool-use-with-claude/08-implem.content:
Implementing multiple turns



                  Course context 



Part of Tool use with Claude — Building with the Claude API (Skilljar)



In plain terms
This lesson turns the multi-turn tool-use loop from the previous lesson into working, complete code as part of the module’s hands-on project. It’s the point where all the earlier pieces — schemas, response handling, sending results, looping — come together into a single program that runs from a user’s question to a final answer, however many tool calls it takes.
Deep dive
Building a correct multi-turn tool loop means being disciplined about a few structural details that are easy to get subtly wrong. Here is a complete, self-contained implementation that ties together everything from “Tool functions” through “Multi-turn conversations with tools”:
import anthropic
import json

client = anthropic.Anthropic()

def get_weather(location: str, unit: str = &quot;fahrenheit&quot;) -&gt; str:
    temp = 68 if unit == &quot;fahrenheit&quot; else 20
    return json.dumps({&quot;location&quot;: location, &quot;temperature&quot;: temp, &quot;unit&quot;: unit, &quot;conditions&quot;: &quot;sunny&quot;})

TOOL_FUNCTIONS = {&quot;get_weather&quot;: get_weather}

tools = [{
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;get_weather&quot;,
    &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Get the current weather in a given location&quot;,
    &quot;input_schema&quot;: {
        &quot;type&quot;: &quot;object&quot;,
        &quot;properties&quot;: {
            &quot;location&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;The city and state, e.g. San Francisco, CA&quot;},
            &quot;unit&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;, &quot;enum&quot;: [&quot;celsius&quot;, &quot;fahrenheit&quot;]}
        },
        &quot;required&quot;: [&quot;location&quot;]
    }
}]

def execute_tool(name: str, tool_input: dict) -&gt; str:
    if name not in TOOL_FUNCTIONS:
        return json.dumps({&quot;error&quot;: f&quot;unknown tool &#039;{name}&#039;&quot;})
    try:
        return TOOL_FUNCTIONS[name](**tool_input)
    except Exception as e:
        return json.dumps({&quot;error&quot;: str(e)})

def run_conversation(user_message: str, max_iterations: int = 10) -&gt; str:
    messages = [{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: user_message}]

    for _ in range(max_iterations):
        response = client.messages.create(
            model=&quot;claude-opus-5&quot;,
            max_tokens=1024,
            tools=tools,
            messages=messages
        )

        if response.stop_reason != &quot;tool_use&quot;:
            return &quot;&quot;.join(b.text for b in response.content if b.type == &quot;text&quot;)

        messages.append({&quot;role&quot;: &quot;assistant&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: response.content})

        tool_results = []
        for block in response.content:
            if block.type == &quot;tool_use&quot;:
                output = execute_tool(block.name, block.input)
                tool_results.append({
                    &quot;type&quot;: &quot;tool_result&quot;,
                    &quot;tool_use_id&quot;: block.id,
                    &quot;content&quot;: output
                })
        messages.append({&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: tool_results})

    return &quot;Stopped: exceeded max tool-call iterations.&quot;

print(run_conversation(&quot;What&#039;s the weather in Boston in celsius?&quot;))
A few implementation choices worth calling out:

A max_iterations safety cap. An unbounded while loop is risky in production — if a tool keeps returning something that makes Claude want to call it again (a bug in the tool, an ambiguous schema, or a model quirk), the loop could run indefinitely, burning tokens and API calls. Capping iterations and returning a clear message when the cap is hit is a standard defensive pattern.
JSON-encoding structured tool output. Returning json.dumps({...}) rather than an ad hoc string gives Claude a consistent, parseable structure and makes it easier for you to log or debug what a tool actually returned.
Wrapping tool execution in try/except. A single malformed input or a downstream API failure shouldn’t crash the whole conversation; catching the exception and reporting it back as tool output (optionally with &quot;is_error&quot;: true, from “Sending tool results”) lets Claude adapt instead of your program dying.
Returning only the text blocks as the final answer. Once stop_reason isn’t &quot;tool_use&quot;, response.content may still include leftover thinking/text blocks; filtering for type == &quot;text&quot; and joining them is the simplest way to extract the user-facing answer.

Debugging a loop like this is mostly about instrumentation: logging response.stop_reason, the tool name/input on each call, and the tool_result content sent back makes it easy to spot exactly where a conversation goes off track — for example, an infinite loop caused by a tool schema that doesn’t match what the tool function actually needs, or a tool_use_id copy-paste bug that silently confuses which result answers which call. With this working end-to-end, the project is ready to be extended to use more than one distinct tool, which is the focus of the next lesson.
Key takeaways

A complete tool loop combines: define tools/functions, call the API, branch on stop_reason, execute and collect tool_results, append them, and repeat.
Add a max_iterations safety cap to avoid runaway loops in production code.
Returning structured (JSON) tool output and wrapping execution in try/except makes the loop more robust and debuggable.
Filter for type == &quot;text&quot; blocks once the loop exits to extract Claude’s final, user-facing answer.

Related lessons
Previous: 07 Multi-turn conversations with tools
Next: 09 Using multiple tools

claude/05-tool-use-with-claude/11-the-te.content:
The text edit tool



                  Course context 



Part of Tool use with Claude — Building with the Claude API (Skilljar)



In plain terms
So far, every tool in this module has been something you built yourself. Anthropic also ships a few ready-made tools you can enable without writing a schema from scratch. The text editor tool lets Claude view, and propose precise edits to, text files — useful for coding assistants and similar workflows — while you still remain responsible for actually reading and writing the file on disk.
Deep dive
The text editor tool is a client tool with a predefined schema — you don’t write an input_schema, you just reference it by its fixed type and name:
tools = [
    {
        &quot;type&quot;: &quot;text_editor_20250728&quot;,
        &quot;name&quot;: &quot;str_replace_based_edit_tool&quot;
    }
]
Despite being predefined by Anthropic, this is still a client-side tool in the same sense as everything else in this module: Claude only produces tool_use requests describing an edit; your code is responsible for actually performing file operations (reading the file, writing changes, returning results) and sending a tool_result back — the mechanics from “Handling message blocks” and “Sending tool results” apply unchanged.
The tool supports a small set of commands, sent as the command field inside input:

view — view the contents of a file (or list a directory).
str_replace — replace an exact string (old_str) with a new string (new_str) in a file at path. This is the most common editing command; it requires old_str to match exactly and uniquely, similar in spirit to how a find-and-replace tool would fail on ambiguous matches.
create — create a new file with given content.
insert — insert text at a specific line number.

Example flow: Claude first requests a view to see the file, then a str_replace to fix an issue it found:
{
  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;tool_use&quot;,
  &quot;id&quot;: &quot;toolu_01PqRsTuVwXyZAbCdEfGh&quot;,
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;str_replace_based_edit_tool&quot;,
  &quot;input&quot;: {
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;str_replace&quot;,
    &quot;path&quot;: &quot;primes.py&quot;,
    &quot;old_str&quot;: &quot;    for num in range(2, limit + 1)&quot;,
    &quot;new_str&quot;: &quot;    for num in range(2, limit + 1):&quot;
  }
}
Your handling code dispatches on input[&quot;command&quot;] rather than on tool name (since there’s only one tool name, str_replace_based_edit_tool, covering every editing operation):
import os

def handle_text_editor_tool(tool_input: dict) -&gt; str:
    command = tool_input[&quot;command&quot;]
    path = tool_input[&quot;path&quot;]

    if command == &quot;view&quot;:
        if os.path.isdir(path):
            return &quot;\n&quot;.join(os.listdir(path))
        with open(path) as f:
            return f.read()

    if command == &quot;str_replace&quot;:
        with open(path) as f:
            content = f.read()
        if tool_input[&quot;old_str&quot;] not in content:
            return f&quot;Error: old_str not found in {path}&quot;
        content = content.replace(tool_input[&quot;old_str&quot;], tool_input[&quot;new_str&quot;], 1)
        with open(path, &quot;w&quot;) as f:
            f.write(content)
        return f&quot;Successfully replaced text in {path}&quot;

    if command == &quot;create&quot;:
        with open(path, &quot;w&quot;) as f:
            f.write(tool_input[&quot;file_text&quot;])
        return f&quot;Created {path}&quot;

    if command == &quot;insert&quot;:
        with open(path) as f:
            lines = f.readlines()
        lines.insert(tool_input[&quot;insert_line&quot;], tool_input[&quot;new_str&quot;] + &quot;\n&quot;)
        with open(path, &quot;w&quot;) as f:
            f.writelines(lines)
        return f&quot;Inserted text into {path}&quot;

    return f&quot;Error: unknown command &#039;{command}&#039;&quot;
This slots into the exact same loop from “Implementing multiple turns”: check stop_reason == &quot;tool_use&quot;, find the tool_use block (its name will be &quot;str_replace_based_edit_tool&quot;), call your handler, and send the return value back as a tool_result with the matching tool_use_id. The main difference from a fully custom tool is that you don’t need to design the schema — Anthropic has already defined what input looks like for each command — but you still own everything about actually touching the filesystem, including safety: validating paths so Claude can’t be tricked into editing files outside an intended directory, and deciding whether edits need human confirmation before being applied.
The tool version string (text_editor_20250728 in the example) matters — different dated versions may support different commands or parameters (for example, an optional max_characters parameter to truncate large file views was added in the text_editor_20250728 version), so check which version pairs with the model you’re using.
Common pitfalls: treating the text editor tool as if Claude edits the file directly (it doesn’t — your handler does the actual I/O); not validating that old_str is unique before replacing, which can silently touch the wrong occurrence; and not sandboxing file paths, which is a real security concern if path ever comes from untrusted input by way of the conversation.
Key takeaways

The text editor tool is referenced by a fixed type (e.g. text_editor_20250728) and name (str_replace_based_edit_tool) — no custom input_schema needed.
It’s still a client tool: Claude only requests edits via commands like view, str_replace, create, and insert; your code performs the actual file I/O.
Dispatch on input[&quot;command&quot;] rather than tool name, since one tool name covers all editing operations.
You’re responsible for path validation and safety — Anthropic’s schema doesn’t sandbox filesystem access for you.

Related lessons
Previous: 10 Fine grained tool calling
Next: 12 The web search tool

claude/06-rag-and-agentic-search/05-impl.content:
Implementing the RAG flow



                  Course context 



Part of RAG and Agentic Search — Building with the Claude API (Skilljar)



In plain terms
This lesson turns the conceptual RAG flow into real, runnable code — chunking actual documents, storing embeddings somewhere searchable, and wiring retrieval up to a Claude API call. It’s the hands-on counterpart to the previous, more theoretical lesson, showing the practical decisions (which library, how to structure the code) that a working prototype requires.
Deep dive
A concrete RAG implementation needs to make a few practical choices beyond the conceptual pipeline: what to use as a vector store, how to structure chunk metadata, and how to keep the ingestion and query code cleanly separated (since, as covered previously, they run at different times and different frequencies).
For a small-to-medium prototype, you don’t need a dedicated vector database server — an in-memory list of embeddings with NumPy for similarity search is often enough, and it makes the mechanics of retrieval fully visible rather than hidden behind a database client. For production scale, you’d swap this for a real vector store (e.g., a managed vector database, or a self-hosted option like FAISS or pgvector) that supports approximate nearest-neighbor search and persistence, but the surrounding pipeline code barely changes — only the storage and search calls do.
A complete minimal implementation, combining chunking, embedding, storage, retrieval, and generation into one script:
import anthropic
import voyageai
import numpy as np

vo = voyageai.Client()
client = anthropic.Anthropic()


def chunk_text(text: str, chunk_size: int = 500, overlap: int = 50) -&gt; list[str]:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Simple fixed-size chunking with overlap, splitting on whitespace.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    words = text.split()
    chunks = []
    step = chunk_size - overlap
    for start in range(0, len(words), step):
        chunk = &quot; &quot;.join(words[start : start + chunk_size])
        if chunk:
            chunks.append(chunk)
    return chunks


class SimpleVectorStore:
    def __init__(self):
        self.chunks: list[str] = []
        self.metadata: list[dict] = []
        self.embeddings: np.ndarray | None = None

    def add_documents(self, documents: list[dict]) -&gt; None:
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Each document is {&quot;text&quot;: ..., &quot;source&quot;: ...}.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        for doc in documents:
            for chunk in chunk_text(doc[&quot;text&quot;]):
                self.chunks.append(chunk)
                self.metadata.append({&quot;source&quot;: doc[&quot;source&quot;]})

        new_embeddings = vo.embed(
            self.chunks, model=&quot;voyage-4&quot;, input_type=&quot;document&quot;
        ).embeddings
        self.embeddings = np.array(new_embeddings)

    def search(self, query: str, top_k: int = 3) -&gt; list[dict]:
        query_embedding = vo.embed(
            [query], model=&quot;voyage-4&quot;, input_type=&quot;query&quot;
        ).embeddings[0]
        scores = np.dot(self.embeddings, query_embedding)
        top_indices = np.argsort(scores)[::-1][:top_k]
        return [
            {&quot;text&quot;: self.chunks[i], &quot;source&quot;: self.metadata[i][&quot;source&quot;], &quot;score&quot;: float(scores[i])}
            for i in top_indices
        ]


def generate_answer(question: str, store: SimpleVectorStore) -&gt; str:
    results = store.search(question, top_k=3)
    context = &quot;\n\n&quot;.join(f&quot;[Source: {r[&#039;source&#039;]}]\n{r[&#039;text&#039;]}&quot; for r in results)

    response = client.messages.create(
        model=&quot;claude-opus-5&quot;,
        max_tokens=512,
        system=(
            &quot;You are a support assistant. Answer using only the provided &quot;
            &quot;documents. Cite the source of any fact you use. If the answer &quot;
            &quot;isn&#039;t in the documents, say you don&#039;t know.\n\n&quot;
            f&quot;Documents:\n{context}&quot;
        ),
        messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: question}],
    )
    return response.content[0].text


# --- Ingestion (run once, or whenever documents change) ---
store = SimpleVectorStore()
store.add_documents([
    {&quot;text&quot;: &quot;To reset your password, go to Settings &gt; Security...&quot;, &quot;source&quot;: &quot;help/password.md&quot;},
    {&quot;text&quot;: &quot;Refunds are processed within 5-7 business days after approval.&quot;, &quot;source&quot;: &quot;help/refunds.md&quot;},
])

# --- Query time (run per user request) ---
print(generate_answer(&quot;How long do refunds take?&quot;, store))
A few implementation details deserve attention. First, keeping metadata (like source) alongside each chunk from the start pays off immediately — it lets the generation step attribute facts to a document, which both improves user trust and sets up the citations feature covered elsewhere in this course. Second, notice that add_documents re-embeds all accumulated chunks every time it’s called in this simplified version; a real implementation would only embed newly added chunks and append to the existing embedding matrix, since re-embedding unchanged text wastes API calls and money. Third, error handling matters more than it looks: if the embedding API call fails partway through a large ingestion batch, you want to be able to resume rather than re-embed everything from scratch — production systems typically embed in smaller batches and track progress.
A common pitfall when first implementing this flow is testing only with a handful of documents where naive fixed-size chunking and top-3 retrieval work fine, then discovering in production that with a large, varied corpus, purely vector-based retrieval misses exact-match queries — a user searching for an exact error code or product SKU may not get a strong semantic match, because embeddings are tuned for meaning, not literal string matching. That gap is exactly what the next lesson, on BM25 lexical search, addresses.
Key takeaways

A working RAG prototype can be built with an in-memory embedding list and NumPy; production systems swap this for a persistent vector store without changing the surrounding pipeline logic.
Keep chunk-level metadata (source, section) from the start — it’s needed for attribution and later for citations.
Only re-embed new or changed chunks in production; re-embedding an entire corpus on every ingestion run is wasteful and costly.
Batch and checkpoint ingestion so a failed embedding call doesn’t force a full restart.
Pure vector search can miss exact-match queries (codes, SKUs, names) because embeddings optimize for semantic similarity, not literal matching — a gap lexical search fills.

Related lessons
04 The full RAG flow
06 BM25 lexical search

claude/10-agents-and-workflows/03-chaini.content:
Chaining workflows



                  Course context 



Part of Agents and workflows — Building with the Claude API (Skilljar)



In plain terms
Chaining means breaking a big task into a series of smaller steps, where each step is its own call to Claude and feeds its answer into the next step — like an assembly line. Each individual step is simpler and easier to get right than trying to do the whole task in one giant prompt, and you can add checkpoints between steps to catch mistakes before they cascade.
Deep dive
Chaining, also called prompt chaining, is the second core workflow pattern from Anthropic’s “Building Effective Agents” guidance. Where parallelization runs independent calls concurrently, chaining runs a sequence of calls where each one depends on the last — call 2 can’t start until call 1 finishes, because call 2’s prompt is built (in part) from call 1’s output.
The motivation is decomposition. A single LLM call asked to do everything at once — say, “read this raw customer feedback, extract the themes, categorize each theme by severity, and write an executive summary” — is asking the model to juggle several distinct kinds of reasoning simultaneously. Breaking that into a chain of three or four calls (extract themes → categorize severity → draft summary → polish tone) means each individual call only has to be good at one narrower thing, which is usually easier for the model to do reliably than the combined task, and easier for a human to review and debug when something goes wrong, since you can inspect the intermediate outputs.
A defining feature of chains, distinct from a plain sequence of calls, is the gate: a check inserted between two steps that verifies the previous step’s output is acceptable before letting the chain continue. A gate can be a simple programmatic assertion (does the output parse as valid JSON? does it contain the expected fields? is it under a length limit?), a rule-based validation function, or even another LLM call whose only job is to judge the previous step’s output (“Does this summary accurately reflect the extracted themes? Answer yes or no.”). Gates are what let a chain fail fast and predictably instead of quietly passing a bad intermediate result all the way to the end, where it’s much more expensive to notice and fix.
import json
from anthropic import Anthropic

client = Anthropic()

def call(prompt: str) -&gt; str:
    response = client.messages.create(
        model=&quot;claude-opus-5&quot;,
        max_tokens=1024,
        messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: prompt}],
    )
    return response.content[0].text

def extract_themes(feedback: str) -&gt; list[str]:
    raw = call(
        f&quot;Extract the main themes from this feedback as a JSON array of strings. &quot;
        f&quot;Respond with ONLY the JSON array.\n\n{feedback}&quot;
    )
    return json.loads(raw)  # will raise if the model didn&#039;t return valid JSON

def gate_themes(themes: list[str]) -&gt; bool:
    # Simple programmatic gate: did we actually get something usable?
    return len(themes) &gt; 0 and all(isinstance(t, str) for t in themes)

def categorize_severity(themes: list[str]) -&gt; str:
    return call(
        f&quot;For each theme below, assign a severity of low/medium/high. &quot;
        f&quot;Themes: {themes}&quot;
    )

def draft_summary(severity_report: str) -&gt; str:
    return call(f&quot;Write a two-paragraph executive summary of this report:\n{severity_report}&quot;)

def run_chain(feedback: str) -&gt; str:
    themes = extract_themes(feedback)
    if not gate_themes(themes):
        raise ValueError(&quot;Theme extraction produced an unusable result — stopping chain&quot;)
    severity_report = categorize_severity(themes)
    return draft_summary(severity_report)
Each function is a link in the chain; run_chain is the fixed sequence a developer wrote, and gate_themes is the checkpoint that stops bad data from propagating into the (more expensive, harder-to-debug) later steps. In a production system the gate might retry the previous step with a corrective prompt instead of raising, but the principle — verify before proceeding — is the same.
Chaining’s core tradeoff is latency and cost versus accuracy and controllability. Each additional link in the chain adds a full round trip to the model, so a five-step chain is going to be noticeably slower and more expensive per request than a single call — but each step, being narrower in scope, is also more likely to be correct, and the intermediate outputs give you visibility you don’t get from one opaque call. The rule of thumb from the “Building Effective Agents” guidance is to add chain steps only when the task decomposition genuinely simplifies each individual step; chaining for its own sake, without a real decomposition benefit, just adds latency.
Chaining connects directly to the next lesson on routing: a chain’s first step is often itself a router — “figure out what kind of request this is” — whose output determines which of several possible next-step prompts gets used. In fact, routing can be thought of as a chain of exactly one classification step followed by a branch, rather than a fixed linear sequence; the two patterns compose naturally, and a real system often chains several steps where one of the early links is a router.
Pitfalls. The most common failure is chaining steps that don’t actually need to be separate — if step 2 never really depends on step 1’s specific content (only on the original input), you’ve paid for an extra round trip with no decomposition benefit, and the two could be merged or parallelized instead. A second pitfall is skipping gates: without a checkpoint, a malformed or wrong intermediate output silently flows downstream and corrupts everything after it, and by the time you notice the final output is wrong, it’s much harder to tell which link in the chain actually failed. A third is an unbounded chain — dynamically deciding to add “one more step” without a hard cap can turn a workflow into something that behaves like a runaway agent loop, defeating the predictability that was the whole point of choosing a workflow in the first place.
Key takeaways

Chaining decomposes one task into a fixed sequence of LLM calls, where each step’s output feeds the next step’s input.
Smaller, narrower steps are generally more accurate than one call trying to do everything, and they’re easier to debug.
Gates — programmatic checks or LLM-judge calls between steps — catch bad intermediate output before it propagates further down the chain.
The tradeoff is added latency and cost per extra link versus the accuracy and visibility gained from decomposition; only chain when decomposition genuinely helps.
A chain’s first step is often a router (next lesson) that decides which downstream path the rest of the chain should take.

